// std/jagent.jtr — `jagent`, an edge agent: configured jobs, a durable run history, and
// a local HTTP API for what it knows.
//
// TIER: `sys`, by dependency. Jobs run through `std/sysproc` (inside a `std/sandbox`
// group, so a timed-out job's whole process tree goes with it), history lives in `std/kv`,
// the API is `std/httpd`, configuration is `std/config` + `std/ini` with the file watched
// through `std/syswatch`. Everything the agent touches outside itself is GIVEN to it — an
// allocator, a `time.Clock`, a `log.Logger`, an `fs.Fs`, a `sysproc.Spawner` — so a test
// hands over its own and reads back exactly what happened.
//
// ─── What it is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A small operational agent for one machine: a few named jobs in an INI file, each a
// shell command with a timeout; `run` executes one and records the outcome durably;
// `serve` exposes health, metrics, the job table and the run history over loopback HTTP,
// runs jobs on request, reloads the file when it changes, and runs any job that declares
// an interval on that schedule. The smallest thing that can grow into a runner for a
// homelab or a build box, built from parts the tree already had.
//
// ─── The configuration is a SCHEMA the file itself declares ───────────────────────────
//
// `std/config` refuses a key nobody declared, and the job names are the file's to choose.
// So a load is two passes: the section headers are scanned first and every `[job.NAME]`
// declares its four keys (`kind`, `command`, `timeout_ms`, `interval_ms`), then `ini.load`
// applies the file against that schema. A typo in a key is therefore an ERROR that names
// its line, not a setting that silently does nothing — which is the property `std/ini`
// exists for, and it holds for keys the module never saw coming.
//
// **A reload applies whole or not at all**, `std/livecfg`'s rule: the new file builds a
// CANDIDATE configuration and job table, and only a candidate with zero faults replaces
// the current one. A file with three good jobs and one bad line changes nothing; the
// faults are kept and rendered with `file:line:col` through `std/diag`, and the previous
// jobs keep running. `generation` counts the loads that changed something.
//
// ─── A run is one atomic history record, or none ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// Every run writes THREE keys in ONE `kv` batch — the record (`run:<seq>`), the job's
// latest pointer (`last:<name>`), and the sequence counter (`seq`) — so a crash can never
// leave the counter ahead of the records or a job pointing at a run that was never
// written. A job that does not exist writes nothing: there is no run to record, and a
// history entry for a name nobody configured would be a lie the next `status` repeats.
// A store that could not be opened does not stop a run from happening — the operator
// asked for it — but the run reports `recorded=false` and the agent counts it, because a
// silently unrecorded run is the failure a history exists to prevent.
//
// The record is JSON (`std/json`'s writer, its one escape table), so the output preview
// can hold anything a command printed, newlines included, and the reader parses it back
// with the same module rather than a second grammar.
//
// ─── A timeout kills the TREE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A command runs through the platform shell, so the child `sysproc` holds is `cmd.exe` or
// `sh` and the work is under it. Killing the shell alone orphans the work — the defect
// `std/plugin` recorded and closed — so a job starts `apart` inside a `sandbox.Group`,
// joins it before it runs an instruction, and a timeout terminates the child, then the
// group, and waits (bounded) for the group to empty. `Run.tree_reaped` reports whether it
// did; it is a measurement, never an assumption.
//
// The output is drained WHILE the child runs, gated on `sysproc.output_ready`, because a
// child that fills its pipe blocks and a parent waiting for it to exit never drains — the
// deadlock `std/sysproc`'s header describes. The wait is charged to a clock the caller can
// SPEND (`blk`), separate from the agent's own clock, for `std/supervise`'s reason: a test
// runs the agent on `time.manual()` so every timestamp and duration it records is a number
// the test chose, while the children are real processes that need real time.
//
// ─── What it does NOT do ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **TLS is the command's, not this module's.** `agent.tls = true` with `tls_cert` and
// `tls_key` is served by `jagent_cli` through `std/httpds` — one request per connection,
// every blocking call bounded — over the same routes and middleware `install` registers;
// this module only checks the setting is complete (`serve_refusal`) and answers
// `tls_supported`. A `tls = true` without both files is REFUSED with the reason rather
// than served in plaintext under a setting that says otherwise.
//
// **One job kind.** `kind = command` is the only value accepted today; the kind is a
// column in the job table and a check at load time, so a second kind is a new constant,
// a new arm in `execute`, and nothing else. No retries, no concurrency (a run blocks the
// API for its duration, bounded by its timeout — stated, and the reason the timeout is
// required to be positive), no job dependencies, no per-job environment or working
// directory (`sysproc.start_piped_at` offers both; nothing here asks for them yet).
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/jagent_demo.jtr     (the transcript is pinned)
// The command:     examples/std/jagent_cli.jtr      (`jagent serve|run|schedule|status|logs`)
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/jagent_test.jtr

import "config"
import "diag"
import "fs"
import "http"
import "httpc"
import "httpd"
import "ini"
import "json"
import "kv"
import "list"
import "log"
import "mem"
import "metrics"
import "sandbox"
import "service"
import "sink"
import "str"
import "sysnet"
import "sysproc"
import "syswatch"
import "time"

// The wall clock, for the ONE thing this module records that a monotonic counter cannot
// give: when a run started, as something a human can read back next week. ISO C's
// `time(NULL)`, aliased because `time` is the clock module's name. Replaceable through
// `set_wall`, so a transcript can pin it.
extern "time.h" fn wall_seconds = "time"(t: cptr) -> i64

// ---- outcomes -----------------------------------------------------------------------

// How a run ended. The first four are RECORDED statuses; the fifth never reaches the
// store, because a job nobody configured has no history to write into.
pub const RUN_SUCCESS: i32 = 0
pub const RUN_FAILURE: i32 = 1        // a non-zero exit, a signal, or a start that failed
pub const RUN_TIMEOUT: i32 = 2        // killed at `timeout_ms`, tree and all
pub const RUN_CONFIG_ERROR: i32 = 3   // the job exists but cannot be run as written
pub const RUN_NO_SUCH_JOB: i32 = 4    // not recorded

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn status_name(s: i32) -> str {
    if s == RUN_SUCCESS { return "success" }
    if s == RUN_FAILURE { return "failure" }
    if s == RUN_TIMEOUT { return "timeout" }
    if s == RUN_CONFIG_ERROR { return "config-error" }
    if s == RUN_NO_SUCH_JOB { return "no-such-job" }
    return "?"
}

// Job kinds. `command` runs through the shell; `http-check` is a GET through `std/httpc`
// whose exit code is the response status against `expect_status`.
pub const JOB_COMMAND: i32 = 0
pub const JOB_UNKNOWN: i32 = 1
pub const JOB_HTTP_CHECK: i32 = 2

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn kind_name(k: i32) -> str {
    if k == JOB_COMMAND { return "command" }
    if k == JOB_HTTP_CHECK { return "http-check" }
    return "unknown"
}

// The most retries a job may declare: a bound on how long one run can take beyond its
// timeout, so a misconfiguration cannot make a run last an hour.
pub const JAGENT_MAX_RETRIES: i64 = 10

// What a load or a reload did.
pub const JA_LOADED: i32 = 0       // applied; the generation advanced
pub const JA_SAME: i32 = 1         // the same bytes as the current configuration; nothing moved
pub const JA_REJECTED: i32 = 2     // faults; the previous configuration stands
pub const JA_UNREADABLE: i32 = 3   // no file; the previous configuration stands
pub const JA_QUIET: i32 = 4        // `poll_config`: the watcher reported nothing

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn load_name(r: i32) -> str {
    if r == JA_LOADED { return "loaded" }
    if r == JA_SAME { return "same" }
    if r == JA_REJECTED { return "rejected" }
    if r == JA_UNREADABLE { return "unreadable" }
    if r == JA_QUIET { return "quiet" }
    return "?"
}

// ---- sizes ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// How much of a command's output a history record keeps. A record is a summary; the
// whole output of a job is the job's business (redirect it), and a store that grew by
// megabytes per run would be the wrong shape for the history of a box.
pub const JAGENT_PREVIEW_CAP: usize = 240
// One read of a job's pipe.
const JAGENT_CHUNK: usize = 4096
// A rendered configuration value may be this long — a command line, in practice.
const JAGENT_VALUE_CAP: usize = 4096
// A rendered fault, a history record, a status line.
const JAGENT_LINE_CAP: usize = 8192
// How long a timed-out job's tree is given to empty after the kill. Its own number, not
// the job's timeout: it bounds a sweep, and the job's budget is already spent.
const JAGENT_REAP_NANOS: i64 = 2000000000
// The slice a drain sleeps for when nothing is ready — `sysproc`'s own floor.
const JAGENT_POLL_NANOS: i64 = 1000000
// How many drain rounds `poll_config` allows the watcher before giving up on convergence.
const JAGENT_DRAIN_ROUNDS: usize = 16
// The default job timeout, when the file does not say.
const JAGENT_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS: i64 = 5000
// The history keys.
const KEY_SEQ: str = "seq"
const KEY_RUN: str = "run:"
const KEY_LAST: str = "last:"

// ---- one run ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What a run reported. `@copy`: scalars only; the output preview and the error text live
// on the agent (`last_output`, `last_error`) because a struct may not hold a `str` and a
// `String` field would make this move-only for no reason.
@copy pub struct Run {
    pub status: i32,
    pub code: i32,            // the exit code when there is one; the signal when killed
    pub seq: i64,             // the history sequence number, 0 when not recorded
    pub started_at: i64,      // wall-clock seconds
    pub duration_ms: i64,     // on the agent's clock
    pub out_bytes: i64,       // every byte the command wrote, not just the preview
    pub recorded: bool,
    pub tree_reaped: bool,    // true when the process group was CONFIRMED empty afterwards
                              // (vacuously true for an http-check: nothing was spawned)
    pub attempts: i32,        // 1 plus the retries taken; the record carries it
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn no_run() -> Run {
    return Run{ status: RUN_NO_SUCH_JOB, code: 0, seq: 0, started_at: 0, duration_ms: 0,
                out_bytes: 0, recorded: false, tree_reaped: false, attempts: 0 }
}

// ---- the agent --------------------------------------------------------------------------

@move pub struct Agent {
    a: Allocator,
    clock: time.Clock,
    wall: fn() -> i64,
    lg: log.Logger,
    lraw: *mut u8,            // the last log record, rendered — a test reads it here
    llen: usize,
    m: metrics.Registry,
    svc: service.Service,
    // configuration
    cfg: config.Config,
    src: String,              // the text of the current configuration
    cfg_name: String,         // its file name, for diagnostics
    faults: String,           // every fault of the LAST load, rendered
    fault_count: i64,
    valid: bool,              // has a configuration ever been applied?
    generation: i64,
    loads: i64,
    rejections: i64,
    // the job table: name and command bytes in two arenas, one column per fact
    names: String,
    nofs: List(i32),
    nlen: List(i32),
    cmds: String,
    cofs: List(i32),
    clen: List(i32),
    kinds: List(i32),
    timeouts: List(i64),      // ms
    intervals: List(i64),     // ms; 0 = not scheduled
    due: List(i64),           // the next scheduled start, on the agent's clock
    expects: List(i64),       // http-check: the status that is success
    cwds: String,             // command: the working directory, "" = inherit
    cwofs: List(i32),
    cwlen: List(i32),
    envs: String,             // command: one NAME=value appended to the environment, "" = none
    enofs: List(i32),
    enlen: List(i32),
    retries: List(i64),       // attempts beyond the first
    backoffs: List(i64),      // ms between attempts
    // groups: a name and the job INDICES it lists, flattened
    gnames: String,
    gnofs: List(i32),
    gnlen: List(i32),
    gjobs: List(i32),
    gofs: List(i32),
    glen: List(i32),
    // retention
    max_runs: i64,            // 0 = keep everything
    compact_after: i64,       // dead bytes that trigger a compaction; 0 = never
    // history
    store: kv.Store,
    store_path: String,
    // the watcher over the configuration's directory
    w: syswatch.Watcher,
    // metric ids
    m_runs: i32,
    m_ok: i32,
    m_fail: i32,
    m_timeout: i32,
    m_cfgerr: i32,
    m_missing: i32,
    m_unrecorded: i32,
    m_http: i32,
    m_unauth: i32,
    m_retries: i32,
    m_pruned: i32,
    m_compactions: i32,
    m_reloads: i32,
    m_dur: i32,
    m_jobs: i32,
    // what the last run left behind
    preview: String,
    why: String,
    last_status: i32,
}

impl Drop for Agent {
    fn drop(mut self) {
        string_free(self.src)
        string_free(self.cfg_name)
        string_free(self.faults)
        string_free(self.names)
        string_free(self.cmds)
        string_free(self.cwds)
        string_free(self.envs)
        string_free(self.gnames)
        string_free(self.store_path)
        string_free(self.preview)
        string_free(self.why)
    }
}

fn host_wall() -> i64 {
    return wall_seconds(null)
}

fn nothing() { }

// An agent with no configuration and no store. `clk` is the agent's own clock — every
// timestamp and duration it records comes from it; `lg` receives one record per run and
// per reload. The service is READY from the start: `run` is work, and a `make` that
// needed a second call before it would accept any would make the CLI's `run` a two-step.
pub fn make(read a: Allocator, take clk: time.Clock, take lg: log.Logger) -> Agent {
    var m: metrics.Registry = metrics.make(a)
    var svc: service.Service = service.make(m)
    var c: time.Clock = clk
    service.ready(svc, c)
    var bounds: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, 6)
    unsafe { (bounds + 0).* = 10 }
    unsafe { (bounds + 1).* = 100 }
    unsafe { (bounds + 2).* = 1000 }
    unsafe { (bounds + 3).* = 10000 }
    unsafe { (bounds + 4).* = 60000 }
    unsafe { (bounds + 5).* = 600000 }
    let bs: []i64 = slice(i64, bounds, 6)
    let m_dur: i32 = metrics.histogram(m, "jagent_run_ms", bs)
    free_ptr(bounds as *mut u8)
    return Agent{
        a: a, clock: c, wall: &host_wall, lg: lg,
        lraw: alloc(u8, JAGENT_LINE_CAP), llen: 0,
        m: m, svc: svc,
        cfg: config.make(a), src: string_new(), cfg_name: string_new(), faults: string_new(),
        fault_count: 0, valid: false, generation: 0, loads: 0, rejections: 0,
        names: string_new(), nofs: list.make(i32, a), nlen: list.make(i32, a),
        cmds: string_new(), cofs: list.make(i32, a), clen: list.make(i32, a),
        kinds: list.make(i32, a), timeouts: list.make(i64, a), intervals: list.make(i64, a),
        due: list.make(i64, a),
        expects: list.make(i64, a),
        cwds: string_new(), cwofs: list.make(i32, a), cwlen: list.make(i32, a),
        envs: string_new(), enofs: list.make(i32, a), enlen: list.make(i32, a),
        retries: list.make(i64, a), backoffs: list.make(i64, a),
        gnames: string_new(), gnofs: list.make(i32, a), gnlen: list.make(i32, a),
        gjobs: list.make(i32, a), gofs: list.make(i32, a), glen: list.make(i32, a),
        max_runs: 0, compact_after: 0,
        store: kv.closed(), store_path: string_new(),
        w: syswatch.closed(),
        m_runs: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_runs_total"),
        m_ok: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_runs_success"),
        m_fail: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_runs_failure"),
        m_timeout: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_runs_timeout"),
        m_cfgerr: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_runs_config_error"),
        m_missing: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_runs_no_such_job"),
        m_unrecorded: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_runs_unrecorded"),
        m_http: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_http_requests"),
        m_unauth: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_http_unauthorized"),
        m_retries: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_run_retries"),
        m_pruned: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_history_pruned"),
        m_compactions: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_history_compactions"),
        m_reloads: metrics.counter(m, "jagent_config_reloads"),
        m_dur: m_dur,
        m_jobs: metrics.gauge(m, "jagent_jobs"),
        preview: string_new(), why: string_new(), last_status: RUN_NO_SUCH_JOB,
    }
}

// Release everything `Drop` cannot: the store, the watcher, the registry, the logger,
// the configuration. The agent is unusable afterwards.
pub fn free(mut ag: Agent) {
    close_store(ag)
    syswatch.close_watch(ag.w) catch nothing()
    config.free(ag.cfg)
    metrics.free(ag.m)
    log.free(ag.lg)
    if ag.lraw != null {
        free_ptr(ag.lraw)
        ag.lraw = null
    }
}

// The wall clock as the agent reads it — the seconds a record's `started_at` is compared to.
pub fn wall_now(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return ag.wall() }

// Replace the wall clock. A transcript that must not depend on the calendar installs a
// function returning a constant.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn set_wall(mut ag: Agent, f: fn() -> i64) {
    ag.wall = f
}

// The agent's clock, read and moved — `advance` is how a test on a manual clock reaches a
// scheduled job's due time.
pub fn now(mut ag: Agent) -> i64 { return time.read_nanos(ag.clock) }
pub fn advance(mut ag: Agent, by: i64) -> bool { return time.advance(ag.clock, by) }

// ---- small renderers ---------------------------------------------------------------------

fn push_i64(mut s: String, v: i64) {
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, 24)
    var b: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, 24)
    var sk: sink.Sink = sink.new()
    sink.put_i64(sk, b, v)
    string_push(s, sink.view(sk, b))
    free_ptr(raw)
}

fn push_bool(mut s: String, v: bool) {
    if v { string_push(s, "true") } else { string_push(s, "false") }
}

// A history sequence number as twelve zero-padded digits, so the keys sort as the runs
// happened and a `run:` prefix scan reads them in order.
fn push_seq(mut s: String, v: i64) {
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, 24)
    var b: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, 24)
    var sk: sink.Sink = sink.new()
    sink.put_i64(sk, b, v)
    let d: str = sink.view(sk, b)
    var k: usize = d.len
    for k < 12 {
        string_push(s, "0")
        k = k + 1
    }
    string_push(s, d)
    free_ptr(raw)
}

// A value as a JSON string — the one escape table in the tree, so a preview with a quote
// or a newline is one field on one line wherever it is rendered.
fn push_quoted(mut s: String, read v: str) {
    let cap: usize = v.len * 6 + 8
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, cap)
    var b: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, cap)
    var sk: sink.Sink = sink.new()
    var w: json.Writer = json.writer()
    json.put_str(w, sk, b, v)
    string_push(s, sink.view(sk, b))
    free_ptr(raw)
}

// Decimal digits only; -1 for anything else (a `seq` value that is not a number is damage).
@no_alloc @no_os fn parse_i64(read s: str) -> i64 {
    if s.len == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
    var acc: i64 = 0
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < s.len {
        let d: i32 = s[i] as i32
        if d < 48 { return 0 - 1 }
        if d > 57 { return 0 - 1 }
        acc = acc * 10 + ((d - 48) as i64)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return acc
}

// The 1-based line and column of byte `off` in `text`.
@no_alloc @no_os fn line_of(read text: str, off: usize) -> i64 {
    var ln: i64 = 1
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < off and i < text.len {
        if (text[i] as i32) == 10 { ln = ln + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return ln
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn col_of(read text: str, off: usize) -> i64 {
    var col: i64 = 1
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < off and i < text.len {
        if (text[i] as i32) == 10 { col = 1 } else { col = col + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return col
}

// The current value of `id`, rendered into `dst`. False when it would not fit.
fn value_of(read c: config.Config, id: i32, mut dst: String) -> bool {
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, JAGENT_VALUE_CAP)
    var b: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, JAGENT_VALUE_CAP)
    var sk: sink.Sink = sink.new()
    config.render_value(sk, b, c, id)
    let over: bool = sink.overflowed(sk)
    if over == false { string_push(dst, sink.view(sk, b)) }
    free_ptr(raw)
    return over == false
}

// ---- the job table ----------------------------------------------------------------------

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn job_count(read ag: Agent) -> usize {
    return list.len(i32, ag.kinds)
}

pub fn job_name(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> read str {
    let v: str = string_view(ag.names)
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return v[0 .. 0] }
    let o: usize = list.get(i32, ag.nofs, i) as usize
    let n: usize = list.get(i32, ag.nlen, i) as usize
    return v[o .. o + n]
}

pub fn job_command(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> read str {
    let v: str = string_view(ag.cmds)
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return v[0 .. 0] }
    let o: usize = list.get(i32, ag.cofs, i) as usize
    let n: usize = list.get(i32, ag.clen, i) as usize
    return v[o .. o + n]
}

pub fn job_kind(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> i32 {
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return JOB_UNKNOWN }
    return list.get(i32, ag.kinds, i)
}

// An http-check's URL — the same column a command job keeps its command in.
pub fn job_url(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> read str { return job_command(ag, i) }

pub fn job_expect_status(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> i64 {
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return 0 }
    return list.get(i64, ag.expects, i)
}

// A command job's working directory ("" inherits) and its one extra `NAME=value` ("" none).
pub fn job_cwd(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> read str {
    let v: str = string_view(ag.cwds)
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return v[0 .. 0] }
    let o: usize = list.get(i32, ag.cwofs, i) as usize
    let n: usize = list.get(i32, ag.cwlen, i) as usize
    return v[o .. o + n]
}

pub fn job_env(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> read str {
    let v: str = string_view(ag.envs)
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return v[0 .. 0] }
    let o: usize = list.get(i32, ag.enofs, i) as usize
    let n: usize = list.get(i32, ag.enlen, i) as usize
    return v[o .. o + n]
}

pub fn job_retries(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> i64 {
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return 0 }
    return list.get(i64, ag.retries, i)
}

pub fn job_backoff_ms(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> i64 {
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return 0 }
    return list.get(i64, ag.backoffs, i)
}

// ---- groups ----

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn group_count(read ag: Agent) -> usize { return list.len(i32, ag.gofs) }

pub fn group_name(read ag: Agent, g: usize) -> read str {
    let v: str = string_view(ag.gnames)
    if g >= group_count(ag) { return v[0 .. 0] }
    let o: usize = list.get(i32, ag.gnofs, g) as usize
    let n: usize = list.get(i32, ag.gnlen, g) as usize
    return v[o .. o + n]
}

pub fn group_size(read ag: Agent, g: usize) -> usize {
    if g >= group_count(ag) { return 0 }
    return list.get(i32, ag.glen, g) as usize
}

// The job INDEX of member `k` of group `g`, in file order.
pub fn group_job(read ag: Agent, g: usize, k: usize) -> usize {
    if k >= group_size(ag, g) { return 0 }
    let o: usize = list.get(i32, ag.gofs, g) as usize
    return list.get(i32, ag.gjobs, o + k) as usize
}

pub fn find_group(read ag: Agent, read name: str) -> i32 {
    var g: usize = 0
    let n: usize = group_count(ag)
    for g < n {
        if str_eq(group_name(ag, g), name) { return g as i32 }
        g = g + 1
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

// ---- retention ----

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn history_max_runs(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return ag.max_runs }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn compact_after_dead_bytes(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return ag.compact_after }

pub fn job_timeout_ms(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> i64 {
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return 0 }
    return list.get(i64, ag.timeouts, i)
}

pub fn job_interval_ms(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> i64 {
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return 0 }
    return list.get(i64, ag.intervals, i)
}

// When job `i` is next due on the agent's clock, or -1 when it is not scheduled.
pub fn job_due(read ag: Agent, i: usize) -> i64 {
    if i >= job_count(ag) { return 0 - 1 }
    if list.get(i64, ag.intervals, i) <= 0 { return 0 - 1 }
    return list.get(i64, ag.due, i)
}

// The index of the job called `name`, or -1. A linear scan: an agent has a handful of
// jobs, and the name is compared once per request.
pub fn find_job(read ag: Agent, read name: str) -> i32 {
    var i: usize = 0
    let n: usize = job_count(ag)
    for i < n {
        if str_eq(job_name(ag, i), name) { return i as i32 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

// ---- the agent section --------------------------------------------------------------------

fn agent_int(read ag: Agent, read key: str) -> i64 {
    return config.get_int(ag.cfg, config.by_name(ag.cfg, key))
}

fn agent_bool(read ag: Agent, read key: str) -> bool {
    return config.get_bool(ag.cfg, config.by_name(ag.cfg, key))
}

// `agent.port`; 0 asks the platform for one.
pub fn port(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return agent_int(ag, "agent.port") }

// Does `agent.host` name the loopback interface? False means every interface (`0.0.0.0`);
// anything else was refused at load.
pub fn host_is_loopback(read ag: Agent) -> bool {
    let id: i32 = config.by_name(ag.cfg, "agent.host")
    return config.value_is(ag.cfg, id, "0.0.0.0") == false
}

pub fn tls_wanted(read ag: Agent) -> bool { return agent_bool(ag, "agent.tls") }

// Whether this build can serve TLS. It can, through `std/httpds` (one request per
// connection, every blocking call bounded — its header says what that costs); the answer
// stays a function so a caller asks rather than assumes.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn tls_supported() -> bool { return true }

// `agent.tls_cert` / `agent.tls_key`, appended to `dst`; false when unset or empty.
pub fn tls_cert(read ag: Agent, mut dst: String) -> bool { return agent_path(ag, "agent.tls_cert", dst) }
pub fn tls_key(read ag: Agent, mut dst: String) -> bool { return agent_path(ag, "agent.tls_key", dst) }

fn agent_path(read ag: Agent, read key: str, mut dst: String) -> bool {
    let id: i32 = config.by_name(ag.cfg, key)
    if id < 0 { return false }
    if config.is_set(ag.cfg, id) == false { return false }
    if config.value_is(ag.cfg, id, "") { return false }
    return value_of(ag.cfg, id, dst)
}

// Why `serve` would refuse to start, or "" when it would not. Asked by the CLI before it
// binds a socket, so a configuration that asks for something the agent cannot honour is
// refused with the reason rather than served under a different contract.
pub fn serve_refusal(read ag: Agent) -> str {
    if ag.valid == false { return "no valid configuration is loaded" }
    if tls_wanted(ag) {
        if tls_supported() == false {
            return "agent.tls = true, but this build cannot serve TLS (docs/jagent.md)"
        }
        var cert: String = string_new()
        var key: String = string_new()
        let both: bool = tls_cert(ag, cert) and tls_key(ag, key)
        string_free(key)
        string_free(cert)
        if both == false {
            return "agent.tls = true, but tls_cert and tls_key are not both set: cannot serve TLS without a certificate and its key (docs/jagent.md)"
        }
    }
    return ""
}

// The path the history lives at: `agent.store`, `jagent.db` by default.
pub fn store_path(read ag: Agent) -> read str { return string_view(ag.store_path) }

// Is the API gated by a bearer token? True when `agent.token` is set and non-empty. There
// is no accessor for the token itself: it is compared (`token_matches`) and rendered as
// `****`, and that is all that can be done with it.
pub fn token_required(read ag: Agent) -> bool {
    if ag.valid == false { return false }
    let id: i32 = config.by_name(ag.cfg, "agent.token")
    if id < 0 { return false }
    if config.is_set(ag.cfg, id) == false { return false }
    return config.value_is(ag.cfg, id, "") == false
}

// Does `presented` equal the configured token? Constant-time in the token's length.
fn token_matches(read ag: Agent, read presented: str) -> bool {
    let id: i32 = config.by_name(ag.cfg, "agent.token")
    if id < 0 { return false }
    return config.value_is_ct(ag.cfg, id, presented)
}

// ---- loading a configuration ---------------------------------------------------------------

@no_alloc @no_os fn name_byte_ok(b: i32) -> bool {
    if b >= 48 and b <= 57 { return true }
    if b >= 65 and b <= 90 { return true }
    if b >= 97 and b <= 122 { return true }
    if b == 45 or b == 95 { return true }
    return false
}

fn fault_at(mut faults: String, read fname: str, read text: str, off: usize, read msg: str) {
    string_push(faults, "error[E-JAGENT]: ")
    string_push(faults, msg)
    string_push(faults, "\n  --> ")
    string_push(faults, fname)
    string_push(faults, ":")
    push_i64(faults, line_of(text, off))
    string_push(faults, ":")
    push_i64(faults, col_of(text, off))
    string_push(faults, "\n")
}

// A candidate job table, built while the file is scanned and thrown away if it is not
// applied. Name bytes in `names`, one row per `[job.NAME]` header, plus the header's byte
// offset so a fault found later can point at it.
struct Cand {
    names: String,
    nofs: List(i32),
    nlen: List(i32),
    hdr: List(i32),
    // `[group.NAME]` headers, the same way
    gnames: String,
    gnofs: List(i32),
    gnlen: List(i32),
    ghdr: List(i32),
}

fn cand_name(read c: Cand, i: usize) -> read str {
    let v: str = string_view(c.names)
    let o: usize = list.get(i32, c.nofs, i) as usize
    let n: usize = list.get(i32, c.nlen, i) as usize
    return v[o .. o + n]
}

fn cand_group(read c: Cand, i: usize) -> read str {
    let v: str = string_view(c.gnames)
    let o: usize = list.get(i32, c.gnofs, i) as usize
    let n: usize = list.get(i32, c.gnlen, i) as usize
    return v[o .. o + n]
}

fn name_ok(read nm: str) -> bool {
    if nm.len == 0 { return false }
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < nm.len {
        if name_byte_ok(nm[i] as i32) == false { return false }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}

// Pass one: the section headers. Every `[job.NAME]` becomes a row; a header that is not a
// job is `std/ini`'s to judge. A name is refused for a byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, because
// the name is also a URL segment and a history key, and for being a duplicate, because
// two sections of one name would race for one set of keys.
fn scan_jobs(read text: str, read fname: str, mut c: Cand, mut faults: String, read a: Allocator) {
    var at: usize = 0
    for at <= text.len {
        var e: usize = at
        for e < text.len {
            if (text[e] as i32) == 10 { break }
            e = e + 1
        }
        let line: str = str.trimmed(text[at .. e])
        if line.len >= 2 {
            if (line[0] as i32) == 91 and (line[line.len - 1] as i32) == 93 {
                let inner: str = line[1 .. line.len - 1]
                if str.has_prefix(inner, "job.") {
                    let nm: str = inner[4 .. inner.len]
                    var fine: bool = nm.len > 0
                    if fine == false {
                        fault_at(faults, fname, text, at, "a job section needs a name: `[job.NAME]`")
                    }
                    var i: usize = 0
                    for i < nm.len {
                        if name_byte_ok(nm[i] as i32) == false {
                            if fine {
                                fault_at(faults, fname, text, at, "a job name may hold only letters, digits, `-` and `_`")
                            }
                            fine = false
                        }
                        i = i + 1
                    }
                    if fine {
                        var k: usize = 0
                        let n: usize = list.len(i32, c.nofs)
                        for k < n {
                            if str_eq(cand_name(c, k), nm) {
                                fault_at(faults, fname, text, at, "this job is declared twice")
                                fine = false
                            }
                            k = k + 1
                        }
                    }
                    if fine {
                        let v0: str = string_view(c.names)
                        list.push(i32, c.nofs, v0.len as i32)
                        list.push(i32, c.nlen, nm.len as i32)
                        list.push(i32, c.hdr, at as i32)
                        string_push(c.names, nm)
                    }
                }
                if str.has_prefix(inner, "group.") {
                    let gm: str = inner[6 .. inner.len]
                    var gfine: bool = name_ok(gm)
                    if gfine == false {
                        fault_at(faults, fname, text, at, "a group section needs a name of letters, digits, `-` and `_`: `[group.NAME]`")
                    }
                    if gfine {
                        var k2: usize = 0
                        let ng: usize = list.len(i32, c.gnofs)
                        for k2 < ng {
                            if str_eq(cand_group(c, k2), gm) {
                                fault_at(faults, fname, text, at, "this group is declared twice")
                                gfine = false
                            }
                            k2 = k2 + 1
                        }
                    }
                    if gfine {
                        let g0: str = string_view(c.gnames)
                        list.push(i32, c.gnofs, g0.len as i32)
                        list.push(i32, c.gnlen, gm.len as i32)
                        list.push(i32, c.ghdr, at as i32)
                        string_push(c.gnames, gm)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        at = e + 1
    }
}

// The schema: the agent section, and four keys per job, with their defaults.
fn declare_all(mut cfg: config.Config, read c: Cand) {
    config.declare(cfg, "agent.host", config.CFG_STR, false)
    config.declare(cfg, "agent.port", config.CFG_INT, false)
    config.declare(cfg, "agent.tls", config.CFG_BOOL, false)
    config.declare(cfg, "agent.tls_cert", config.CFG_STR, false)
    config.declare(cfg, "agent.tls_key", config.CFG_STR, false)
    config.declare(cfg, "agent.store", config.CFG_STR, false)
    // SECRET by declaration: `std/config` renders it as `****` on every path, so nothing
    // that prints the configuration can print the token by forgetting to.
    config.declare(cfg, "agent.token", config.CFG_STR, true)
    config.declare(cfg, "agent.history_max_runs", config.CFG_INT, false)
    config.declare(cfg, "agent.compact_after_dead_bytes", config.CFG_INT, false)
    config.apply(cfg, "agent.host", "127.0.0.1", config.CFG_DEFAULT)
    config.apply(cfg, "agent.port", "0", config.CFG_DEFAULT)
    config.apply(cfg, "agent.tls", "false", config.CFG_DEFAULT)
    config.apply(cfg, "agent.store", "jagent.db", config.CFG_DEFAULT)
    config.apply(cfg, "agent.history_max_runs", "0", config.CFG_DEFAULT)
    config.apply(cfg, "agent.compact_after_dead_bytes", "0", config.CFG_DEFAULT)
    var t: String = string_new()
    push_i64(t, JAGENT_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS)
    var i: usize = 0
    let n: usize = list.len(i32, c.nofs)
    for i < n {
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "kind", config.CFG_STR, "command")
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "command", config.CFG_STR, "")
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "timeout_ms", config.CFG_INT, string_view(t))
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "interval_ms", config.CFG_INT, "0")
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "url", config.CFG_STR, "")
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "expect_status", config.CFG_INT, "200")
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "cwd", config.CFG_STR, "")
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "env", config.CFG_STR, "")
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "retries", config.CFG_INT, "0")
        declare_job_key(cfg, c, i, "backoff_ms", config.CFG_INT, "0")
        i = i + 1
    }
    var g: usize = 0
    let ng: usize = list.len(i32, c.gnofs)
    for g < ng {
        declare_group_key(cfg, c, g)
        g = g + 1
    }
    string_free(t)
}

// Declare `job.<name>.<leaf>` of kind `k`, with `dflt` as its default when non-empty.
fn declare_job_key(mut cfg: config.Config, read c: Cand, i: usize, read leaf: str, k: i32, read dflt: str) {
    var key: String = string_new()
    string_push(key, "job.")
    string_push(key, cand_name(c, i))
    string_push(key, ".")
    string_push(key, leaf)
    config.declare(cfg, string_view(key), k, false)
    if dflt.len > 0 { config.apply(cfg, string_view(key), dflt, config.CFG_DEFAULT) }
    string_free(key)
}

// A group has one key: `group.<name>.jobs`, a comma-separated list of job names.
fn declare_group_key(mut cfg: config.Config, read c: Cand, g: usize) {
    var key: String = string_new()
    string_push(key, "group.")
    string_push(key, cand_group(c, g))
    string_push(key, ".jobs")
    config.declare(cfg, string_view(key), config.CFG_STR, false)
    string_free(key)
}

fn group_key(read c: Cand, g: usize, read cfg: config.Config) -> i32 {
    var k: String = string_new()
    string_push(k, "group.")
    string_push(k, cand_group(c, g))
    string_push(k, ".jobs")
    let id: i32 = config.by_name(cfg, string_view(k))
    string_free(k)
    return id
}

// Call `f` for every comma-separated, trimmed, non-empty item of `list_text`... spelled as
// a count-and-index pair instead, because a closure is not a thing this subset has: the
// number of items, and the k-th item.
fn list_item_count(read s: str) -> usize {
    // A never-pushed `String` views as a str with no bytes behind it; slicing that, even
    // `[0 .. 0]`, is what the runtime's boundary check refuses.
    if s.len == 0 { return 0 }
    var n: usize = 0
    var at: usize = 0
    for at <= s.len {
        var e: usize = at
        for e < s.len {
            if (s[e] as i32) == 44 { break }
            e = e + 1
        }
        let item: str = str.trimmed(s[at .. e])
        if item.len > 0 { n = n + 1 }
        at = e + 1
    }
    return n
}

fn list_item(read s: str, want: usize) -> read str {
    if s.len == 0 { return s }
    var n: usize = 0
    var at: usize = 0
    for at <= s.len {
        var e: usize = at
        for e < s.len {
            if (s[e] as i32) == 44 { break }
            e = e + 1
        }
        let item: str = str.trimmed(s[at .. e])
        if item.len > 0 {
            if n == want { return item }
            n = n + 1
        }
        at = e + 1
    }
    return s[0 .. 0]
}

// Every `std/ini` problem, rendered with a caret against the file's own text.
fn render_ini_faults(read a: Allocator, read fname: str, read text: str, read x: ini.Ini, mut faults: String) -> i64 {
    let total: usize = ini.problem_count(x)
    if total == 0 { return 0 }
    var m: diag.SourceMap = diag.map_new(a)
    let fid: diag.FileId = diag.add_file(m, fname, text)
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, JAGENT_LINE_CAP)
    var b: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, JAGENT_LINE_CAP)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < total {
        let sp: diag.Span = diag.span(fid, ini.problem_start(x, i), ini.problem_end(x, i))
        var d: diag.Diagnostic = diag.new_error(a, "E-INI", ini.code_name(ini.problem_code(x, i)))
        let cf: i32 = ini.problem_cfg(x, i)
        if cf >= 0 {
            diag.primary(d, sp, config.outcome_name(cf))
        } else {
            diag.primary(d, sp, "here")
        }
        var sk: sink.Sink = sink.new()
        diag.render(sk, b, m, d, diag.plain())
        if sink.overflowed(sk) == false { string_push(faults, sink.view(sk, b)) }
        i = i + 1
    }
    free_ptr(raw)
    return total as i64
}

// The one key of job `i` in `c`, looked up in `cfg`.
fn job_key(read c: Cand, i: usize, read leaf: str, read cfg: config.Config) -> i32 {
    var k: String = string_new()
    string_push(k, "job.")
    string_push(k, cand_name(c, i))
    string_push(k, ".")
    string_push(k, leaf)
    let id: i32 = config.by_name(cfg, string_view(k))
    string_free(k)
    return id
}

// Pass three: what the schema cannot say. A command must be present and non-empty, a
// kind must be one this agent runs, a timeout must be positive (a zero timeout is a job
// that is always killed, and a job that blocks the API for ever is what the timeout
// exists to rule out), an interval may not be negative, and the host must be one the
// server can bind.
fn check_semantics(read c: Cand, read cfg: config.Config, read fname: str, read text: str, mut faults: String) -> i64 {
    var n_faults: i64 = 0
    let hid: i32 = config.by_name(cfg, "agent.host")
    var host_ok: bool = false
    if config.value_is(cfg, hid, "127.0.0.1") { host_ok = true }
    if config.value_is(cfg, hid, "localhost") { host_ok = true }
    if config.value_is(cfg, hid, "0.0.0.0") { host_ok = true }
    if host_ok == false {
        fault_at(faults, fname, text, 0, "agent.host must be 127.0.0.1, localhost or 0.0.0.0")
        n_faults = n_faults + 1
    }
    let pid: i32 = config.by_name(cfg, "agent.port")
    let pv: i64 = config.get_int(cfg, pid)
    if pv < 0 or pv > 65535 {
        fault_at(faults, fname, text, 0, "agent.port must be 0..65535")
        n_faults = n_faults + 1
    }
    if config.get_int(cfg, config.by_name(cfg, "agent.history_max_runs")) < 0 {
        fault_at(faults, fname, text, 0, "agent.history_max_runs may not be negative (0 keeps everything)")
        n_faults = n_faults + 1
    }
    if config.get_int(cfg, config.by_name(cfg, "agent.compact_after_dead_bytes")) < 0 {
        fault_at(faults, fname, text, 0, "agent.compact_after_dead_bytes may not be negative (0 never compacts)")
        n_faults = n_faults + 1
    }
    var i: usize = 0
    let n: usize = list.len(i32, c.nofs)
    for i < n {
        let hdr: usize = list.get(i32, c.hdr, i) as usize
        let kid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "kind", cfg)
        let is_cmd: bool = config.value_is(cfg, kid, "command")
        let is_http: bool = config.value_is(cfg, kid, "http-check")
        if is_cmd == false and is_http == false {
            fault_at(faults, fname, text, hdr, "unknown job kind; `command` and `http-check` are supported")
            n_faults = n_faults + 1
        }
        if is_cmd {
            let cid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "command", cfg)
            if key_is_set(cfg, cid) == false {
                fault_at(faults, fname, text, hdr, "a command job needs a non-empty `command`")
                n_faults = n_faults + 1
            }
            let eid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "env", cfg)
            if key_is_set(cfg, eid) {
                var ev: String = string_new()
                value_of(cfg, eid, ev)
                let evs: str = string_view(ev)
                let eq: isize = str.index_of(evs, "=")
                if eq <= 0 {
                    fault_at(faults, fname, text, hdr, "`env` must be one `NAME=value`")
                    n_faults = n_faults + 1
                }
                string_free(ev)
            }
        }
        if is_http {
            let uid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "url", cfg)
            var fine_url: bool = key_is_set(cfg, uid)
            if fine_url {
                var uv: String = string_new()
                value_of(cfg, uid, uv)
                fine_url = str.has_prefix(string_view(uv), "http://")
                string_free(uv)
            }
            if fine_url == false {
                fault_at(faults, fname, text, hdr, "an http-check needs a `url` starting with `http://` (https is not checked; see docs/jagent.md)")
                n_faults = n_faults + 1
            }
            let xid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "expect_status", cfg)
            let xv: i64 = config.get_int(cfg, xid)
            if xv < 100 or xv > 599 {
                fault_at(faults, fname, text, hdr, "`expect_status` must be an HTTP status, 100..599")
                n_faults = n_faults + 1
            }
        }
        let tid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "timeout_ms", cfg)
        if config.get_int(cfg, tid) <= 0 {
            fault_at(faults, fname, text, hdr, "`timeout_ms` must be positive")
            n_faults = n_faults + 1
        }
        let iid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "interval_ms", cfg)
        if config.get_int(cfg, iid) < 0 {
            fault_at(faults, fname, text, hdr, "`interval_ms` may not be negative")
            n_faults = n_faults + 1
        }
        let rid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "retries", cfg)
        let rv: i64 = config.get_int(cfg, rid)
        if rv < 0 or rv > JAGENT_MAX_RETRIES {
            fault_at(faults, fname, text, hdr, "`retries` must be 0..10")
            n_faults = n_faults + 1
        }
        let bid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "backoff_ms", cfg)
        if config.get_int(cfg, bid) < 0 {
            fault_at(faults, fname, text, hdr, "`backoff_ms` may not be negative")
            n_faults = n_faults + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    // Groups: every member is a configured job, the list is not empty, and a group does
    // not share a name with a job — the two are addressed by different commands, and one
    // name meaning two things is the confusion the check exists to rule out.
    var g: usize = 0
    let ng: usize = list.len(i32, c.gnofs)
    for g < ng {
        let ghdr: usize = list.get(i32, c.ghdr, g) as usize
        var same: bool = false
        var j: usize = 0
        for j < n {
            if str_eq(cand_name(c, j), cand_group(c, g)) { same = true }
            j = j + 1
        }
        if same {
            fault_at(faults, fname, text, ghdr, "a group may not share its name with a job")
            n_faults = n_faults + 1
        }
        let gid: i32 = group_key(c, g, cfg)
        var members: String = string_new()
        if key_is_set(cfg, gid) { value_of(cfg, gid, members) }
        let mv: str = string_view(members)
        let cnt: usize = list_item_count(mv)
        if cnt == 0 {
            // ASCII only: the faults are counted with `str.count_of`, which slices at byte
            // offsets and asserts on a multi-byte character (an ellipsis cost an afternoon).
            fault_at(faults, fname, text, ghdr, "a group needs `jobs = name, name, ...` with at least one job")
            n_faults = n_faults + 1
        }
        var k: usize = 0
        for k < cnt {
            let item: str = list_item(mv, k)
            var found: bool = false
            var j2: usize = 0
            for j2 < n {
                if str_eq(cand_name(c, j2), item) { found = true }
                j2 = j2 + 1
            }
            if found == false {
                fault_at(faults, fname, text, ghdr, "a group names a job that is not configured")
                n_faults = n_faults + 1
            }
            k = k + 1
        }
        string_free(members)
        g = g + 1
    }
    return n_faults
}

// Set, and not to the empty string.
fn key_is_set(read cfg: config.Config, id: i32) -> bool {
    if id < 0 { return false }
    if config.is_set(cfg, id) == false { return false }
    return config.value_is(cfg, id, "") == false
}

// Replace the live job table from the applied candidate. Scheduled jobs start their
// first interval NOW, on the agent's clock — a reload resets every schedule, which is
// stated rather than hidden: the alternative, carrying a due time across a reload that
// may have changed the interval, is a rule with more cases than callers.
fn adopt(mut ag: Agent, read c: Cand) {
    let t: i64 = time.read_nanos(ag.clock)
    string_free(ag.names)
    ag.names = string_new()
    string_free(ag.cmds)
    ag.cmds = string_new()
    list.truncate(i32, ag.nofs, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.nlen, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.cofs, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.clen, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.kinds, 0)
    list.truncate(i64, ag.timeouts, 0)
    list.truncate(i64, ag.intervals, 0)
    list.truncate(i64, ag.due, 0)
    list.truncate(i64, ag.expects, 0)
    string_free(ag.cwds)
    ag.cwds = string_new()
    list.truncate(i32, ag.cwofs, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.cwlen, 0)
    string_free(ag.envs)
    ag.envs = string_new()
    list.truncate(i32, ag.enofs, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.enlen, 0)
    list.truncate(i64, ag.retries, 0)
    list.truncate(i64, ag.backoffs, 0)
    var i: usize = 0
    let n: usize = list.len(i32, c.nofs)
    for i < n {
        let nm: str = cand_name(c, i)
        let v0: str = string_view(ag.names)
        list.push(i32, ag.nofs, v0.len as i32)
        list.push(i32, ag.nlen, nm.len as i32)
        string_push(ag.names, nm)
        let kid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "kind", ag.cfg)
        var kind: i32 = JOB_COMMAND
        if config.value_is(ag.cfg, kid, "http-check") { kind = JOB_HTTP_CHECK }
        // The command column holds the command, or the URL for an http-check.
        let v1: str = string_view(ag.cmds)
        let start: usize = v1.len
        if kind == JOB_HTTP_CHECK {
            value_of(ag.cfg, job_key(c, i, "url", ag.cfg), ag.cmds)
        } else {
            value_of(ag.cfg, job_key(c, i, "command", ag.cfg), ag.cmds)
        }
        let v2: str = string_view(ag.cmds)
        list.push(i32, ag.cofs, start as i32)
        list.push(i32, ag.clen, (v2.len - start) as i32)
        list.push(i32, ag.kinds, kind)
        list.push(i64, ag.timeouts, config.get_int(ag.cfg, job_key(c, i, "timeout_ms", ag.cfg)))
        let iv: i64 = config.get_int(ag.cfg, job_key(c, i, "interval_ms", ag.cfg))
        list.push(i64, ag.intervals, iv)
        list.push(i64, ag.due, t + iv * 1000000)
        list.push(i64, ag.expects, config.get_int(ag.cfg, job_key(c, i, "expect_status", ag.cfg)))
        let c0: str = string_view(ag.cwds)
        let cstart: usize = c0.len
        let cwid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "cwd", ag.cfg)
        if key_is_set(ag.cfg, cwid) { value_of(ag.cfg, cwid, ag.cwds) }
        let c1: str = string_view(ag.cwds)
        list.push(i32, ag.cwofs, cstart as i32)
        list.push(i32, ag.cwlen, (c1.len - cstart) as i32)
        let e0: str = string_view(ag.envs)
        let estart: usize = e0.len
        let eid: i32 = job_key(c, i, "env", ag.cfg)
        if key_is_set(ag.cfg, eid) { value_of(ag.cfg, eid, ag.envs) }
        let e1: str = string_view(ag.envs)
        list.push(i32, ag.enofs, estart as i32)
        list.push(i32, ag.enlen, (e1.len - estart) as i32)
        list.push(i64, ag.retries, config.get_int(ag.cfg, job_key(c, i, "retries", ag.cfg)))
        list.push(i64, ag.backoffs, config.get_int(ag.cfg, job_key(c, i, "backoff_ms", ag.cfg)))
        i = i + 1
    }
    // Groups: names, and each member resolved to its job index — the check guaranteed it.
    string_free(ag.gnames)
    ag.gnames = string_new()
    list.truncate(i32, ag.gnofs, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.gnlen, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.gjobs, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.gofs, 0)
    list.truncate(i32, ag.glen, 0)
    var g: usize = 0
    let ng: usize = list.len(i32, c.gnofs)
    for g < ng {
        let gm: str = cand_group(c, g)
        let g0: str = string_view(ag.gnames)
        list.push(i32, ag.gnofs, g0.len as i32)
        list.push(i32, ag.gnlen, gm.len as i32)
        string_push(ag.gnames, gm)
        var members: String = string_new()
        value_of(ag.cfg, group_key(c, g, ag.cfg), members)
        let mv: str = string_view(members)
        let cnt: usize = list_item_count(mv)
        list.push(i32, ag.gofs, list.len(i32, ag.gjobs) as i32)
        list.push(i32, ag.glen, cnt as i32)
        var k: usize = 0
        for k < cnt {
            let item: str = list_item(mv, k)
            var j: usize = 0
            for j < n {
                if str_eq(cand_name(c, j), item) { list.push(i32, ag.gjobs, j as i32) }
                j = j + 1
            }
            k = k + 1
        }
        string_free(members)
        g = g + 1
    }
    metrics.set_gauge(ag.m, ag.m_jobs, n as i64)
    ag.max_runs = config.get_int(ag.cfg, config.by_name(ag.cfg, "agent.history_max_runs"))
    ag.compact_after = config.get_int(ag.cfg, config.by_name(ag.cfg, "agent.compact_after_dead_bytes"))
    string_free(ag.store_path)
    ag.store_path = string_new()
    value_of(ag.cfg, config.by_name(ag.cfg, "agent.store"), ag.store_path)
}

fn note_load(mut ag: Agent, r: i32, faults: i64) {
    if log.begin(ag.lg, log.LOG_INFO, "config") {
        log.kv_str(ag.lg, "file", string_view(ag.cfg_name))
        log.kv_str(ag.lg, "outcome", load_name(r))
        log.kv_i64(ag.lg, "jobs", job_count(ag) as i64)
        log.kv_i64(ag.lg, "faults", faults)
        log.kv_i64(ag.lg, "generation", ag.generation)
        ship_log(ag)
    }
}

// Load a configuration from `text`, named `fname` for diagnostics. The candidate is built
// whole — schema, file, semantic checks — and applied only when it has no faults; the
// faults of the last load are kept either way (`faults`, `fault_count`). Identical bytes
// to the current configuration answer `JA_SAME` and touch nothing.
pub fn load_text(mut ag: Agent, read fname: str, read text: str) -> i32 {
    if ag.valid {
        if str_eq(string_view(ag.src), text) { return JA_SAME }
    }
    string_free(ag.cfg_name)
    ag.cfg_name = string_new()
    string_push(ag.cfg_name, fname)

    var faults: String = string_new()
    var c: Cand = Cand{ names: string_new(), nofs: list.make(i32, ag.a), nlen: list.make(i32, ag.a), hdr: list.make(i32, ag.a),
                        gnames: string_new(), gnofs: list.make(i32, ag.a), gnlen: list.make(i32, ag.a), ghdr: list.make(i32, ag.a) }
    scan_jobs(text, fname, c, faults, ag.a)
    var cfg: config.Config = config.make(ag.a)
    declare_all(cfg, c)
    var x: ini.Ini = ini.make(ag.a)
    ini.load(x, cfg, text, config.CFG_FILE)
    var n_faults: i64 = render_ini_faults(ag.a, fname, text, x, faults)
    let vf: str = string_view(faults)
    if vf.len > 0 { n_faults = n_faults + 1 }
    n_faults = check_semantics(c, cfg, fname, text, faults)
    // Every fault above is one rendered block; count them by their marker rather than
    // by summing three sources that could each miss one.
    n_faults = str.count_of(string_view(faults), "error[") as i64
    ini.free(x)

    string_free(ag.faults)
    ag.faults = faults
    ag.fault_count = n_faults
    ag.loads = ag.loads + 1
    if n_faults > 0 {
        config.free(cfg)
        string_free(c.names)
        string_free(c.gnames)
        ag.rejections = ag.rejections + 1
            note_load(ag, JA_REJECTED, n_faults)
            return JA_REJECTED
    }
    config.free(ag.cfg)
    ag.cfg = cfg
    adopt(ag, c)
    string_free(c.names)
    string_free(ag.src)
    ag.src = string_new()
    string_push(ag.src, text)
    ag.valid = true
    ag.generation = ag.generation + 1
    metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_reloads, 1)
    note_load(ag, JA_LOADED, 0)
    return JA_LOADED
}

// Load the file at `path` through `f`. An absent file is `JA_UNREADABLE` and changes
// nothing.
pub fn load_file(mut ag: Agent, mut f: fs.Fs, read path: str) -> i32 {
    if fs.present(f, path) == false {
        string_free(ag.cfg_name)
        ag.cfg_name = string_new()
        string_push(ag.cfg_name, path)
        string_free(ag.faults)
        ag.faults = string_new()
        string_push(ag.faults, "error[E-JAGENT]: cannot read `")
        string_push(ag.faults, path)
        string_push(ag.faults, "`\n")
        ag.fault_count = 1
        ag.loads = ag.loads + 1
        ag.rejections = ag.rejections + 1
        return JA_UNREADABLE
    }
    var text: String = fs.open_text(f, path)
    let r: i32 = load_text(ag, path, string_view(text))
    string_free(text)
    return r
}

// Re-read the file the current configuration came from.
pub fn reload(mut ag: Agent, mut f: fs.Fs) -> i32 {
    var p: String = string_new()
    string_push(p, string_view(ag.cfg_name))
    let r: i32 = load_file(ag, f, string_view(p))
    string_free(p)
    return r
}

pub fn is_valid(read ag: Agent) -> bool { return ag.valid }
pub fn generation(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return ag.generation }
pub fn loads(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return ag.loads }
pub fn rejections(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return ag.rejections }
pub fn fault_count(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return ag.fault_count }
pub fn faults(read ag: Agent) -> read str { return string_view(ag.faults) }
pub fn config_name(read ag: Agent) -> read str { return string_view(ag.cfg_name) }

// ---- watching the configuration --------------------------------------------------------------

// Watch `dir` — the directory the configuration file sits in — so `poll_config` can
// re-read the file when anything under it changes. False when the watcher could not open.
pub fn watch(mut ag: Agent, mut f: fs.Fs, read dir: str) -> bool {
    syswatch.close_watch(ag.w) catch nothing()
    ag.w = syswatch.open_dir(f, ag.a, dir) catch syswatch.closed()
    return syswatch.is_open(ag.w)
}

pub fn watching(read ag: Agent) -> bool { return syswatch.is_open(ag.w) }

// One pass: drain the watcher (bounded, never blocking) and re-read the file if it
// reported anything. A closed watcher answers `JA_QUIET` for ever; a change to some other
// file under the directory answers `JA_SAME`, the cheap re-read.
pub fn poll_config(mut ag: Agent, mut f: fs.Fs) -> i32 {
    if syswatch.is_open(ag.w) == false { return JA_QUIET }
    var evraw: *mut syswatch.Change = alloc(syswatch.Change, 8)
    var evs: []syswatch.Change = slice(syswatch.Change, evraw, 8)
    var nraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, 512)
    var names: []u8 = slice(u8, nraw, 512)
    var seen: usize = 0
    var got: usize = 1
    var rounds: usize = 0
    for got > 0 and rounds < JAGENT_DRAIN_ROUNDS {
        got = syswatch.drain(ag.w, evs, names) catch 0
        seen = seen + got
        rounds = rounds + 1
    }
    free_ptr(nraw)
    free_ptr(evraw as *mut u8)
    if seen == 0 { return JA_QUIET }
    return reload(ag, f)
}

// ---- the store ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Open the history at `store_path` (creating it), through `f`. False when it could not be
// opened — a read-only or denied handle, a file that is not a store — and every run until
// it is reports `recorded=false`.
pub fn open_store(mut ag: Agent, mut f: fs.Fs) -> bool {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) { return true }
    let sp: str = string_view(ag.store_path)
    if sp.len == 0 { return false }
    kv.open(f, ag.a, sp, ag.store) catch nothing()
    return kv.is_open(ag.store)
}

pub fn close_store(mut ag: Agent) {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) { kv.close(ag.store) }
}

pub fn store_is_open(read ag: Agent) -> bool { return kv.is_open(ag.store) }

// Flush the store to the device now. False when it is closed or the sync failed — the
// question `jagent doctor` asks, separately from whether the file opens.
pub fn sync_store(mut ag: Agent) -> bool {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false { return false }
    var fine: bool = true
    kv.sync(ag.store) catch { fine = false }
    return fine
}

// The last run of `name`, as the JSON record it was stored as, appended to `dst`. False
// when the store is closed, the job has never run, or its pointer names a record that is
// not there. The structured form of `render_last`, for a reader that wants the fields
// (`std/jagent_ops` builds the operator summary from it) rather than the line.
pub fn last_record(mut ag: Agent, read name: str, mut dst: String) -> bool {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false { return false }
    var lk: String = string_new()
    string_push(lk, KEY_LAST)
    string_push(lk, name)
    if kv.has(ag.store, string_view(lk)) == false {
        string_free(lk)
        return false
    }
    var rk: String = string_new()
    string_push(rk, KEY_RUN)
    string_push(rk, kv.get(ag.store, string_view(lk)))
    string_free(lk)
    let found: bool = kv.has(ag.store, string_view(rk))
    if found { string_push(dst, kv.get(ag.store, string_view(rk))) }
    string_free(rk)
    return found
}

// A `key = value` line whose key is one the schema declares secret.
fn line_is_secret(read line: str) -> bool {
    let eq: isize = str.index_of(line, "=")
    if eq < 0 { return false }
    return str_eq(str.trimmed(line[0 .. eq as usize]), "token")
}

// The configuration in force, as `jagent config show` prints it: the file's own text, with
// the value of every line whose key the schema declares secret replaced by `****` —
// `std/config`'s spelling, so a reader of either sees the same mark. Empty until a load has
// applied. The file is not re-read: this is the text the agent is running.
pub fn render_config(read ag: Agent, mut dst: String) {
    let text: str = string_view(ag.src)
    var at: usize = 0
    for at < text.len {
        var e: usize = at
        for e < text.len {
            if (text[e] as i32) == 10 { break }
            e = e + 1
        }
        let line: str = text[at .. e]
        if line_is_secret(line) {
            string_push(dst, "token = ****")
        } else {
            string_push(dst, line)
        }
        if e < text.len { string_push(dst, "\n") }
        at = e + 1
    }
}

// The sequence number of the last recorded run, 0 when there is none.
pub fn last_seq(read ag: Agent) -> i64 {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false { return 0 }
    if kv.has(ag.store, KEY_SEQ) == false { return 0 }
    let v: i64 = parse_i64(kv.get(ag.store, KEY_SEQ))
    if v < 0 { return 0 }
    return v
}

// ---- running a job ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The preview of the last run's output, and the last run's error text (empty when there
// was none). Views into the agent, valid until the next run.
pub fn last_output(read ag: Agent) -> read str { return string_view(ag.preview) }
pub fn last_error(read ag: Agent) -> read str { return string_view(ag.why) }

fn set_error(mut ag: Agent, read msg: str) {
    string_free(ag.why)
    ag.why = string_new()
    string_push(ag.why, msg)
}

fn ship_log(mut ag: Agent) {
    var lb: []u8 = slice(u8, ag.lraw, JAGENT_LINE_CAP)
    var s: sink.Sink = sink.new()
    log.end(ag.lg, s, lb)
    ag.llen = sink.written(s)
}

// The last record the agent's logger shipped, as rendered. What a buffered writer
// received; a test reads it here.
pub fn last_log(read ag: Agent) -> str {
    let lb: []u8 = slice(u8, ag.lraw, JAGENT_LINE_CAP)
    return from_utf8(lb[0 .. ag.llen])
}

fn note_run(mut ag: Agent, read name: str, read r: Run) {
    if log.begin(ag.lg, log.LOG_INFO, "job run") {
        log.kv_str(ag.lg, "job", name)
        log.kv_str(ag.lg, "status", status_name(r.status))
        log.kv_i64(ag.lg, "code", r.code as i64)
        log.kv_i64(ag.lg, "ms", r.duration_ms)
        log.kv_i64(ag.lg, "seq", r.seq)
        log.kv_bool(ag.lg, "recorded", r.recorded)
        ship_log(ag)
    }
}

// Write the run as ONE batch: the record, the job's latest pointer, the counter.
fn persist(mut ag: Agent, read name: str, mut r: Run) {
    r.recorded = false
    r.seq = 0
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false {
        metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_unrecorded, 1)
        return
    }
    let seq: i64 = last_seq(ag) + 1
    var key: String = string_new()
    string_push(key, KEY_RUN)
    push_seq(key, seq)
    var lastk: String = string_new()
    string_push(lastk, KEY_LAST)
    string_push(lastk, name)
    var seqv: String = string_new()
    push_i64(seqv, seq)
    var seqp: String = string_new()
    push_seq(seqp, seq)

    // The record, through `std/json`'s writer so the preview is escaped by the one table.
    let pv: str = string_view(ag.preview)
    let ev: str = string_view(ag.why)
    let cap: usize = 256 + name.len + pv.len * 6 + ev.len * 6
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, cap)
    var b: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, cap)
    var sk: sink.Sink = sink.new()
    var w: json.Writer = json.writer()
    json.begin_object(w, sk, b)
    json.key(w, sk, b, "seq")
    json.put_i64(w, sk, b, seq)
    json.key(w, sk, b, "job")
    json.put_str(w, sk, b, name)
    json.key(w, sk, b, "status")
    json.put_str(w, sk, b, status_name(r.status))
    json.key(w, sk, b, "code")
    json.put_i64(w, sk, b, r.code as i64)
    json.key(w, sk, b, "started_at")
    json.put_i64(w, sk, b, r.started_at)
    json.key(w, sk, b, "duration_ms")
    json.put_i64(w, sk, b, r.duration_ms)
    json.key(w, sk, b, "out_bytes")
    json.put_i64(w, sk, b, r.out_bytes)
    json.key(w, sk, b, "attempts")
    json.put_i64(w, sk, b, r.attempts as i64)
    json.key(w, sk, b, "output")
    json.put_str(w, sk, b, pv)
    json.key(w, sk, b, "error")
    json.put_str(w, sk, b, ev)
    json.end_object(w, sk, b)

    var wrote: bool = false
    if sink.overflowed(sk) == false {
        var bt: kv.Batch = kv.batch()
        kv.batch_put(bt, string_view(key), sink.view(sk, b))
        kv.batch_put(bt, string_view(lastk), string_view(seqp))
        kv.batch_put(bt, KEY_SEQ, string_view(seqv))
        wrote = true
        kv.commit(ag.store, bt) catch { wrote = false }
        // **Synced before it is reported recorded.** A committed batch sits in the C
        // library's buffer until a sync or a close, and a `jagent serve` that is killed —
        // the ordinary end of a service — would take every run since the last close with
        // it. Measured: the CLI test's API run was answered `recorded=true` and was not in
        // the file after the kill. A run is rare and a history is the point, so the device
        // flush is paid per run, and `recorded` means what a reader of the file will find.
        if wrote { kv.sync(ag.store) catch { wrote = false } }
    }
    free_ptr(raw)
    string_free(seqp)
    string_free(seqv)
    string_free(lastk)
    string_free(key)
    if wrote {
        r.recorded = true
        r.seq = seq
    } else {
        metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_unrecorded, 1)
    }
}

fn count_status(mut ag: Agent, s: i32) {
    if s == RUN_SUCCESS { metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_ok, 1) }
    if s == RUN_FAILURE { metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_fail, 1) }
    if s == RUN_TIMEOUT { metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_timeout, 1) }
    if s == RUN_CONFIG_ERROR { metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_cfgerr, 1) }
}

// Copy up to the preview's remaining room from `chunk`.
fn take_preview(mut ag: Agent, pv: *mut u8, mut pn: usize, read chunk: []u8) -> usize {
    var n: usize = pn
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < chunk.len and n < JAGENT_PREVIEW_CAP {
        unsafe { (pv + n).* = chunk[i] }
        n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    }
    return n
}

// The preview bytes as text. A cut that split a multi-byte character is trimmed back to
// the last whole one; bytes that were never text read as a marker rather than a trap.
fn fix_preview(mut ag: Agent, pv: *mut u8, pn: usize) {
    string_free(ag.preview)
    ag.preview = string_new()
    var n: usize = pn
    var tries: usize = 0
    for tries < 4 {
        let r = try_from_utf8(slice(u8, pv, n))
        if is_err(r) == false {
            string_push(ag.preview, unwrap(r))
            return
        }
        if n == 0 { break }
        n = n - 1
        tries = tries + 1
    }
    string_push(ag.preview, "<output is not utf-8>")
}

// Run job `ji`: start it apart in a group, drain its output under the timeout, reap it,
// sweep the group, and report. `blk` is the clock the wait is charged to.
fn execute(mut ag: Agent, mut sp: sysproc.Spawner, mut blk: time.Clock, ji: usize) -> Run {
    var r: Run = no_run()
    r.status = RUN_FAILURE
    r.started_at = ag.wall()
    r.attempts = 1
    let t0: i64 = time.read_nanos(ag.clock)
    string_free(ag.preview)
    ag.preview = string_new()
    set_error(ag, "")
    let timeout_ns: i64 = job_timeout_ms(ag, ji) * 1000000

    var g: sandbox.Group = sandbox.no_group()
    if sandbox.open_group(g) == false {
        set_error(ag, "could not open a process group")
        r.duration_ms = (time.read_nanos(ag.clock) - t0) / 1000000
        return r
    }
    var cmd: String = string_new()
    string_push(cmd, job_command(ag, ji))
    // The working directory and the one extra variable are the process's at creation, which
    // is the only time it can be given them (`sysproc`'s argument); "" inherits.
    var cwd: String = string_new()
    string_push(cwd, job_cwd(ag, ji))
    var env1: String = string_new()
    string_push(env1, job_env(ag, ji))
    var ch: sysproc.Child = sysproc.start_piped_at(sp, string_view(cmd), string_view(cwd), string_view(env1), true) catch sysproc.no_child()
    string_free(env1)
    string_free(cwd)
    string_free(cmd)
    if sysproc.is_live(ch) == false {
        sandbox.release_group(g)
        if sysproc.can_start(sp) {
            if job_cwd(ag, ji).len > 0 {
                var e0: String = string_new()
                string_push(e0, "could not start the command (cwd `")
                string_push(e0, job_cwd(ag, ji))
                string_push(e0, "` may not exist)")
                set_error(ag, string_view(e0))
                string_free(e0)
            } else {
                set_error(ag, "could not start the command")
            }
        } else {
            set_error(ag, "the spawner refused to start a process")
        }
        r.duration_ms = (time.read_nanos(ag.clock) - t0) / 1000000
        return r
    }
    if sandbox.join_group(g, ch) == false {
        // A child that could not be grouped is not left running ungrouped — the outcome
        // the group exists to rule out, and cheap here: on Windows it is still suspended.
        sysproc.terminate(ch)
        sysproc.wait(ch)
        sysproc.release(ch)
        sandbox.release_group(g)
        set_error(ag, "could not place the command in a process group")
        r.duration_ms = (time.read_nanos(ag.clock) - t0) / 1000000
        return r
    }
    sysproc.resume(ch)
    // No stdin. A filter would otherwise wait for EOF that never comes.
    sysproc.close_input(ch)

    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, JAGENT_CHUNK)
    var buf: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, JAGENT_CHUNK)
    var pv: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, JAGENT_PREVIEW_CAP)
    var pn: usize = 0
    var waited: i64 = 0
    var timed_out: bool = false
    var reading: bool = true
    for reading {
        if sysproc.output_ready(ch) {
            let n: i64 = sysproc.read_output(ch, buf)
            if n <= 0 {
                reading = false
            } else {
                r.out_bytes = r.out_bytes + n
                pn = take_preview(ag, pv, pn, buf[0 .. n as usize])
            }
        } else {
            if waited >= timeout_ns {
                timed_out = true
                reading = false
            } else {
                var sl: i64 = JAGENT_POLL_NANOS
                let left: i64 = timeout_ns - waited
                if sl > left { sl = left }
                waited = waited + time.wait(blk, sl)
            }
        }
    }
    fix_preview(ag, pv, pn)
    free_ptr(pv)
    free_ptr(raw)

    var st: sysproc.Status = sysproc.last_status(ch)
    if timed_out {
        // The child first, so the status reported is its own; then the tree.
        sysproc.terminate(ch)
        st = sysproc.wait_or_kill(ch, blk, 0)
    } else {
        var left2: i64 = timeout_ns - waited
        if left2 < 0 { left2 = 0 }
        st = sysproc.wait_or_kill(ch, blk, left2)
    }
    var gone: bool = sandbox.group_empty(g)
    if gone == false {
        sandbox.terminate_group(g)
        gone = sandbox.wait_group_empty(g, blk, JAGENT_REAP_NANOS)
    }
    sandbox.release_group(g)
    r.tree_reaped = gone

    r.code = sysproc.code_of(st)
    if timed_out {
        r.status = RUN_TIMEOUT
        var e: String = string_new()
        string_push(e, "timed out after ")
        push_i64(e, job_timeout_ms(ag, ji))
        string_push(e, " ms")
        set_error(ag, string_view(e))
        string_free(e)
    } else {
        if sysproc.is_exited(st) and r.code == 0 {
            r.status = RUN_SUCCESS
        } else {
            r.status = RUN_FAILURE
            var e2: String = string_new()
            if sysproc.is_signalled(st) {
                string_push(e2, "killed by signal ")
                push_i64(e2, r.code as i64)
            } else {
                string_push(e2, "exit code ")
                push_i64(e2, r.code as i64)
            }
            set_error(ag, string_view(e2))
            string_free(e2)
        }
    }
    r.duration_ms = (time.read_nanos(ag.clock) - t0) / 1000000
    return r
}

// ---- an http-check ----

const JAGENT_HTTP_CAP: usize = 8192

// Split `http://host[:port]/path` into its host and path; returns the port (80 when
// absent), or -1 when the URL is not that shape.
fn split_url(read url: str, mut host: String, mut path: String) -> i64 {
    if str.has_prefix(url, "http://") == false { return 0 - 1 }
    let rest: str = url[7 .. url.len]
    var e: usize = 0
    for e < rest.len {
        if (rest[e] as i32) == 47 { break }
        e = e + 1
    }
    let hp: str = rest[0 .. e]
    if e < rest.len { string_push(path, rest[e .. rest.len]) } else { string_push(path, "/") }
    let colon: isize = str.index_of(hp, ":")
    var port: i64 = 80
    if colon >= 0 {
        string_push(host, hp[0 .. colon as usize])
        let ps: str = hp[(colon as usize) + 1 .. hp.len]
        if ps.len == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
        port = 0
        var i: usize = 0
        for i < ps.len {
            let d: i64 = (ps[i] as i64) - 48
            if d < 0 or d > 9 { return 0 - 1 }
            port = port * 10 + d
            i = i + 1
        }
        if port < 1 or port > 65535 { return 0 - 1 }
    } else {
        string_push(host, hp)
    }
    let hv: str = string_view(host)
    if hv.len == 0 { return 0 - 1 }
    return port
}

// `localhost` or a dotted quad, as a host-order address; -1 for anything else. There is
// no resolver in the tree, and an edge agent's checks are of things it can name by
// address — stated in the docs rather than answered with a hang.
fn ipv4_of(read host: str) -> i64 {
    if str_eq(host, "localhost") { return 2130706433 }
    var parts: i64 = 0
    var acc: i64 = 0
    var v: i64 = 0
    var digits: usize = 0
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < host.len {
        let b: i64 = host[i] as i64
        if b == 46 {
            if digits == 0 or acc > 255 { return 0 - 1 }
            v = v * 256 + acc
            parts = parts + 1
            acc = 0
            digits = 0
        } else {
            if b < 48 or b > 57 { return 0 - 1 }
            acc = acc * 10 + (b - 48)
            digits = digits + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if digits == 0 or acc > 255 or parts != 3 { return 0 - 1 }
    return v * 256 + acc
}

// One GET through `std/httpc`: success when the status is `expect_status`, failure with
// the status when it is not, timeout when nothing came back within `timeout_ms` (the
// socket's receive and send are bounded by it), failure with the reason when the request
// could not be made. The preview is `status=N expected=M`; there is no process, so the
// tree is vacuously reaped.
fn execute_http(mut ag: Agent, mut blk: time.Clock, ji: usize) -> Run {
    var r: Run = no_run()
    r.status = RUN_FAILURE
    r.started_at = ag.wall()
    r.attempts = 1
    r.tree_reaped = true
    let t0: i64 = time.read_nanos(ag.clock)
    string_free(ag.preview)
    ag.preview = string_new()
    set_error(ag, "")
    var host: String = string_new()
    var path: String = string_new()
    let port: i64 = split_url(job_url(ag, ji), host, path)
    var ip: i64 = 0 - 1
    if port > 0 { ip = ipv4_of(string_view(host)) }
    if port <= 0 or ip < 0 {
        r.status = RUN_CONFIG_ERROR
        set_error(ag, "url must be http://<ipv4-or-localhost>[:port]/path")
        string_free(path)
        string_free(host)
        r.duration_ms = (time.read_nanos(ag.clock) - t0) / 1000000
        return r
    }
    let timeout_ms: i64 = job_timeout_ms(ag, ji)
    var net: sysnet.Net = sysnet.host()
    var cl: httpc.Client = httpc.dial(net, sysnet.addr(ip as u32, port as u16), JAGENT_HTTP_CAP)
    if httpc.is_open(cl) == false {
        var e: String = string_new()
        string_push(e, "could not connect to ")
        string_push(e, string_view(host))
        string_push(e, ":")
        push_i64(e, port)
        set_error(ag, string_view(e))
        string_free(e)
    } else {
        let sock: sysnet.Socket = sysnet.adopt(httpc.fd(cl))
        sysnet.set_io_timeout(sock, timeout_ms)
        // The wait is real whatever the agent's clock says, so it is measured on the
        // caller's — the same two clocks a command job keeps apart.
        let tb0: i64 = time.read_nanos(blk)
        httpc.request(cl, "GET", string_view(path), "", "")
        let o: i32 = httpc.read_response(cl)
        let spent_ms: i64 = (time.read_nanos(blk) - tb0) / 1000000
        if o == http.HTTP_OK {
            let st: i64 = httpc.status(cl) as i64
            let want: i64 = job_expect_status(ag, ji)
            string_push(ag.preview, "status=")
            push_i64(ag.preview, st)
            string_push(ag.preview, " expected=")
            push_i64(ag.preview, want)
            let pv: str = string_view(ag.preview)
            r.out_bytes = pv.len as i64
            r.code = st as i32
            if st == want {
                r.status = RUN_SUCCESS
            } else {
                var e2: String = string_new()
                string_push(e2, "unexpected status ")
                push_i64(e2, st)
                set_error(ag, string_view(e2))
                string_free(e2)
            }
        } else {
            if spent_ms >= timeout_ms {
                r.status = RUN_TIMEOUT
                var e3: String = string_new()
                string_push(e3, "timed out after ")
                push_i64(e3, timeout_ms)
                string_push(e3, " ms")
                set_error(ag, string_view(e3))
                string_free(e3)
            } else {
                var e4: String = string_new()
                string_push(e4, "no response (")
                string_push(e4, http.outcome_name(o))
                string_push(e4, ")")
                set_error(ag, string_view(e4))
                string_free(e4)
            }
        }
        httpc.hangup(cl)
    }
    sysnet.shutdown(net)
    string_free(path)
    string_free(host)
    r.duration_ms = (time.read_nanos(ag.clock) - t0) / 1000000
    return r
}

// One attempt of job `ji`, by its kind.
fn attempt(mut ag: Agent, mut sp: sysproc.Spawner, mut blk: time.Clock, ji: usize) -> Run {
    if job_kind(ag, ji) == JOB_HTTP_CHECK { return execute_http(ag, blk, ji) }
    return execute(ag, sp, blk, ji)
}

// Run the job called `name` now, and record it. Every recorded status counts as one unit
// of the service's work, accepted before and completed after — which is what makes a
// draining agent refuse a run rather than take one it will not finish. A job with
// `retries` is attempted again after `backoff_ms` on the caller's clock until it succeeds
// or the attempts are spent; ONE record is written, carrying the attempts, the last
// attempt's status and output, the first attempt's start and the whole duration.
pub fn run_job(mut ag: Agent, mut sp: sysproc.Spawner, mut blk: time.Clock, read name: str) -> Run {
    var r: Run = no_run()
    let ji: i32 = find_job(ag, name)
    if ji < 0 {
        metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_missing, 1)
        set_error(ag, "no such job")
        string_free(ag.preview)
        ag.preview = string_new()
        ag.last_status = RUN_NO_SUCH_JOB
        return r
    }
    if service.accept(ag.svc, ag.m) == false {
        r.status = RUN_CONFIG_ERROR
        set_error(ag, "the agent is not accepting work")
        ag.last_status = r.status
        return r
    }
    let kind: i32 = job_kind(ag, ji as usize)
    if kind != JOB_COMMAND and kind != JOB_HTTP_CHECK {
        r.status = RUN_CONFIG_ERROR
        r.started_at = ag.wall()
        r.attempts = 1
        set_error(ag, "unknown job kind")
        string_free(ag.preview)
        ag.preview = string_new()
    } else {
        let t_first: i64 = time.read_nanos(ag.clock)
        let max_attempts: i64 = 1 + job_retries(ag, ji as usize)
        var attempts: i64 = 0
        var started_at: i64 = 0
        var done: bool = false
        for done == false {
            attempts = attempts + 1
            r = attempt(ag, sp, blk, ji as usize)
            if attempts == 1 { started_at = r.started_at }
            if r.status == RUN_SUCCESS or attempts >= max_attempts {
                done = true
            } else {
                metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_retries, 1)
                // The backoff, in slices, on the clock the caller spends.
                var left: i64 = job_backoff_ms(ag, ji as usize) * 1000000
                for left > 0 {
                    var sl: i64 = 50000000
                    if sl > left { sl = left }
                    left = left - time.wait(blk, sl)
                }
            }
        }
        r.attempts = attempts as i32
        r.started_at = started_at
        r.duration_ms = (time.read_nanos(ag.clock) - t_first) / 1000000
    }
    metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_runs, 1)
    count_status(ag, r.status)
    metrics.observe(ag.m, ag.m_dur, r.duration_ms)
    persist(ag, name, r)
    service.completed(ag.svc, ag.m, r.status == RUN_SUCCESS)
    note_run(ag, name, r)
    ag.last_status = r.status
    return r
}

// One scheduler step: run every job whose interval has come due on the agent's clock,
// and schedule its next. Returns how many ran. A job with no interval never runs here.
pub fn tick(mut ag: Agent, mut sp: sysproc.Spawner, mut blk: time.Clock) -> i64 {
    let t: i64 = time.read_nanos(ag.clock)
    var ran: i64 = 0
    var i: usize = 0
    let n: usize = job_count(ag)
    for i < n {
        let iv: i64 = list.get(i64, ag.intervals, i)
        if iv > 0 {
            if list.get(i64, ag.due, i) <= t {
                var nm: String = string_new()
                string_push(nm, job_name(ag, i))
                run_job(ag, sp, blk, string_view(nm))
                string_free(nm)
                list.set(i64, ag.due, i, t + iv * 1000000)
                ran = ran + 1
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return ran
}

// ---- the service's two questions ------------------------------------------------------------------

pub fn is_ready(read ag: Agent) -> bool { return service.is_ready(ag.svc) }
pub fn is_live(read ag: Agent) -> bool { return service.is_live(ag.svc) }

// Stop accepting runs; finish what is in flight. From an API request, a signal, or a test.
pub fn begin_drain(mut ag: Agent) -> bool { return service.begin_drain(ag.svc) }

// Stop, and say why: `service.SVC_CLEAN`, `SVC_ABANDONED` or `SVC_FAILED`.
pub fn halt(mut ag: Agent) -> i32 { return service.halt(ag.svc, ag.m, ag.clock) }

pub fn runs_total(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return metrics.current(ag.m, ag.m_runs) }
pub fn runs_unrecorded(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return metrics.current(ag.m, ag.m_unrecorded) }
pub fn http_requests(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return metrics.current(ag.m, ag.m_http) }
pub fn http_unauthorized(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return metrics.current(ag.m, ag.m_unauth) }
pub fn retries_total(read ag: Agent) -> i64 { return metrics.current(ag.m, ag.m_retries) }

// ---- rendering ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The service's health line: `phase=… ready=… live=… inflight=…`.
pub fn render_health(mut ag: Agent, mut dst: String) {
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, 256)
    var b: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, 256)
    var sk: sink.Sink = sink.new()
    service.render_health(sk, b, ag.svc)
    string_push(dst, sink.view(sk, b))
    free_ptr(raw)
}

// Every metric, in name order.
pub fn render_metrics(mut ag: Agent, mut dst: String) -> bool {
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, JAGENT_LINE_CAP)
    var b: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, JAGENT_LINE_CAP)
    var sk: sink.Sink = sink.new()
    metrics.render(sk, b, ag.m)
    let whole: bool = sink.overflowed(sk) == false
    if whole { string_push(dst, sink.view(sk, b)) }
    free_ptr(raw)
    return whole
}

// One run record from its stored JSON, as one line. False when the value is not a record
// this module wrote — damage, reported rather than skipped silently.
fn render_record(read ag: Agent, read v: str, mut dst: String) -> bool {
    var d: json.Doc = json.parse(ag.a, v)
    if json.parsed(d) == false { return false }
    let root: usize = json.root(d) as usize
    let seq: isize = json.get(d, root, "seq")
    let job: isize = json.get(d, root, "job")
    let st: isize = json.get(d, root, "status")
    let code: isize = json.get(d, root, "code")
    let at: isize = json.get(d, root, "started_at")
    let ms: isize = json.get(d, root, "duration_ms")
    let ob: isize = json.get(d, root, "out_bytes")
    let outn: isize = json.get(d, root, "output")
    let er: isize = json.get(d, root, "error")
    if seq < 0 or job < 0 or st < 0 or code < 0 or at < 0 or ms < 0 or outn < 0 or er < 0 { return false }
    string_push(dst, "#")
    push_i64(dst, json.as_i64(d, seq as usize))
    string_push(dst, " job=")
    string_push(dst, json.as_str(d, job as usize))
    string_push(dst, " status=")
    string_push(dst, json.as_str(d, st as usize))
    string_push(dst, " code=")
    push_i64(dst, json.as_i64(d, code as usize))
    string_push(dst, " started_at=")
    push_i64(dst, json.as_i64(d, at as usize))
    string_push(dst, " duration_ms=")
    push_i64(dst, json.as_i64(d, ms as usize))
    if ob >= 0 {
        string_push(dst, " out_bytes=")
        push_i64(dst, json.as_i64(d, ob as usize))
    }
    // Attempts appear when there was more than one: a single attempt is the common case
    // and the line stays as it always was.
    let atn: isize = json.get(d, root, "attempts")
    if atn >= 0 {
        if json.as_i64(d, atn as usize) > 1 {
            string_push(dst, " attempts=")
            push_i64(dst, json.as_i64(d, atn as usize))
        }
    }
    string_push(dst, " output=")
    push_quoted(dst, json.as_str(d, outn as usize))
    let ev: str = json.as_str(d, er as usize)
    if ev.len > 0 {
        string_push(dst, " error=")
        push_quoted(dst, ev)
    }
    string_push(dst, "\n")
    return true
}

// Does stored record `v` belong to `name`? Parsed rather than substring-matched, so a job
// whose name is a prefix of another's (`db` and `db-backup`) filters exactly.
fn record_is_for(read ag: Agent, read v: str, read name: str) -> bool {
    var d: json.Doc = json.parse(ag.a, v)
    if json.parsed(d) == false { return false }
    let job: isize = json.get(d, json.root(d) as usize, "job")
    if job < 0 { return false }
    return str_eq(json.as_str(d, job as usize), name)
}

// The most recent `limit` run records — of one job when `name` is non-empty, of every job
// otherwise — oldest first, one line each. Returns how many were rendered.
pub fn render_logs(mut ag: Agent, read name: str, limit: usize, mut dst: String) -> usize {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false { return 0 }
    var hits: List(i64) = list.make(i64, ag.a)
    let n: usize = kv.slots(ag.store)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < n {
        if kv.slot_live(ag.store, i) {
            let k: str = kv.slot_key(ag.store, i)
            if str.has_prefix(k, KEY_RUN) {
                var keep: bool = true
                if name.len > 0 { keep = record_is_for(ag, kv.slot_val(ag.store, i), name) }
                if keep { list.push(i64, hits, i as i64) }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let total: usize = list.len(i64, hits)
    var from: usize = 0
    if total > limit { from = total - limit }
    var shown: usize = 0
    var j: usize = from
    for j < total {
        let slot: usize = list.get(i64, hits, j) as usize
        if render_record(ag, kv.slot_val(ag.store, slot), dst) { shown = shown + 1 }
        j = j + 1
    }
    list.free(i64, hits)
    return shown
}

// The last run of `name`, as a record line, or "none" — through the `last:` pointer, so
// it costs two lookups whatever the history's size.
fn render_last(mut ag: Agent, read name: str, mut dst: String) {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false {
        string_push(dst, "none (store closed)\n")
        return
    }
    var lk: String = string_new()
    string_push(lk, KEY_LAST)
    string_push(lk, name)
    if kv.has(ag.store, string_view(lk)) == false {
        string_free(lk)
        string_push(dst, "none\n")
        return
    }
    var rk: String = string_new()
    string_push(rk, KEY_RUN)
    string_push(rk, kv.get(ag.store, string_view(lk)))
    string_free(lk)
    if kv.has(ag.store, string_view(rk)) == false {
        string_free(rk)
        string_push(dst, "none (pointer to a missing record)\n")
        return
    }
    if render_record(ag, kv.get(ag.store, string_view(rk)), dst) == false {
        string_push(dst, "none (damaged record)\n")
    }
    string_free(rk)
}

// One job's line for a listing.
fn render_job_line(read ag: Agent, i: usize, mut dst: String) {
    string_push(dst, job_name(ag, i))
    string_push(dst, " kind=")
    string_push(dst, kind_name(job_kind(ag, i)))
    string_push(dst, " timeout_ms=")
    push_i64(dst, job_timeout_ms(ag, i))
    string_push(dst, " interval_ms=")
    push_i64(dst, job_interval_ms(ag, i))
    string_push(dst, "\n")
}

// Every configured job, one line each, in file order.
pub fn render_jobs(read ag: Agent, mut dst: String) {
    var i: usize = 0
    let n: usize = job_count(ag)
    for i < n {
        render_job_line(ag, i, dst)
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// One job in full: its definition and its last run. False for a job that does not exist.
pub fn render_job(mut ag: Agent, read name: str, mut dst: String) -> bool {
    let ji: i32 = find_job(ag, name)
    if ji < 0 { return false }
    let i: usize = ji as usize
    string_push(dst, "name=")
    string_push(dst, name)
    string_push(dst, "\nkind=")
    string_push(dst, kind_name(job_kind(ag, i)))
    if job_kind(ag, i) == JOB_HTTP_CHECK {
        string_push(dst, "\nurl=")
        push_quoted(dst, job_url(ag, i))
        string_push(dst, "\nexpect_status=")
        push_i64(dst, job_expect_status(ag, i))
    } else {
        string_push(dst, "\ncommand=")
        push_quoted(dst, job_command(ag, i))
        if job_cwd(ag, i).len > 0 {
            string_push(dst, "\ncwd=")
            push_quoted(dst, job_cwd(ag, i))
        }
        if job_env(ag, i).len > 0 {
            string_push(dst, "\nenv=")
            push_quoted(dst, job_env(ag, i))
        }
    }
    string_push(dst, "\ntimeout_ms=")
    push_i64(dst, job_timeout_ms(ag, i))
    string_push(dst, "\ninterval_ms=")
    push_i64(dst, job_interval_ms(ag, i))
    if job_retries(ag, i) > 0 {
        string_push(dst, "\nretries=")
        push_i64(dst, job_retries(ag, i))
        string_push(dst, "\nbackoff_ms=")
        push_i64(dst, job_backoff_ms(ag, i))
    }
    string_push(dst, "\nlast=")
    render_last(ag, name, dst)
    return true
}

// Every group: `name jobs=a, b, c`, one line each, in file order.
pub fn render_groups(read ag: Agent, mut dst: String) {
    var g: usize = 0
    let n: usize = group_count(ag)
    for g < n {
        string_push(dst, group_name(ag, g))
        string_push(dst, " jobs=")
        var k: usize = 0
        let sz: usize = group_size(ag, g)
        for k < sz {
            if k > 0 { string_push(dst, ", ") }
            string_push(dst, job_name(ag, group_job(ag, g, k)))
            k = k + 1
        }
        string_push(dst, "\n")
        g = g + 1
    }
}

// Run every member of group `name`, in file order, each recorded as its own run — there is
// no group record, because a group is a way of asking, not a thing that ran. The summary
// goes to `dst`: `group=NAME jobs=N success=S failure=F`, then one indented run line per
// member. Returns the failures, or -1 for a group that does not exist.
pub fn run_group(mut ag: Agent, mut sp: sysproc.Spawner, mut blk: time.Clock, read name: str, mut dst: String) -> i64 {
    let gi: i32 = find_group(ag, name)
    if gi < 0 { return 0 - 1 }
    let g: usize = gi as usize
    var lines: String = string_new()
    var fine: i64 = 0
    var bad: i64 = 0
    var k: usize = 0
    let sz: usize = group_size(ag, g)
    for k < sz {
        var nm: String = string_new()
        string_push(nm, job_name(ag, group_job(ag, g, k)))
        let r: Run = run_job(ag, sp, blk, string_view(nm))
        if r.status == RUN_SUCCESS { fine = fine + 1 } else { bad = bad + 1 }
        string_push(lines, "  ")
        render_run(ag, string_view(nm), r, lines)
        string_free(nm)
        k = k + 1
    }
    string_push(dst, "group=")
    string_push(dst, name)
    string_push(dst, " jobs=")
    push_i64(dst, sz as i64)
    string_push(dst, " success=")
    push_i64(dst, fine)
    string_push(dst, " failure=")
    push_i64(dst, bad)
    string_push(dst, "\n")
    string_push(dst, string_view(lines))
    string_free(lines)
    return bad
}

// ---- retention ----

// What `maintain` did.
@copy pub struct Maint {
    pub pruned: i64,       // run records deleted
    pub compacted: bool,
    pub reclaimed: i64,    // dead bytes the compaction reclaimed
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn no_maint() -> Maint {
    return Maint{ pruned: 0, compacted: false, reclaimed: 0 }
}

// Is `key` (a `run:<seq>` key) some job's latest run? Those are kept whatever the bound,
// so a `last:` pointer never dangles and `status` never says "missing record".
fn is_latest_of_any(read ag: Agent, read key: str) -> bool {
    var i: usize = 0
    let n: usize = job_count(ag)
    for i < n {
        var lk: String = string_new()
        string_push(lk, KEY_LAST)
        string_push(lk, job_name(ag, i))
        var hit: bool = false
        if kv.has(ag.store, string_view(lk)) {
            var rk: String = string_new()
            string_push(rk, KEY_RUN)
            string_push(rk, kv.get(ag.store, string_view(lk)))
            hit = str_eq(string_view(rk), key)
            string_free(rk)
        }
        string_free(lk)
        if hit { return true }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return false
}

// How many run records are live.
pub fn history_runs(read ag: Agent) -> i64 {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false { return 0 }
    var n: i64 = 0
    var i: usize = 0
    let total: usize = kv.slots(ag.store)
    for i < total {
        if kv.slot_live(ag.store, i) {
            if str.has_prefix(kv.slot_key(ag.store, i), KEY_RUN) { n = n + 1 }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return n
}

pub fn history_dead_bytes(read ag: Agent) -> i64 {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false { return 0 }
    return kv.dead_bytes(ag.store)
}

pub fn history_live_bytes(read ag: Agent) -> i64 {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false { return 0 }
    return kv.live_bytes(ag.store)
}

// Apply the retention policy: delete the OLDEST run records beyond `history_max_runs` that
// are not any job's latest (one batch, synced), then compact when the dead bytes have
// reached `compact_after_dead_bytes`. Sequence numbers are never reused — `seq` is a
// counter, not a count — so `logs` and `last:` stay exact. Nothing to do answers zeros.
pub fn maintain(mut ag: Agent, mut f: fs.Fs) -> Maint {
    var m: Maint = no_maint()
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false { return m }
    if ag.max_runs > 0 {
        var excess: i64 = history_runs(ag) - ag.max_runs
        if excess > 0 {
            var bt: kv.Batch = kv.batch()
            var n_del: i64 = 0
            var i: usize = 0
            let total: usize = kv.slots(ag.store)
            for i < total and excess > 0 {
                if kv.slot_live(ag.store, i) {
                    let k: str = kv.slot_key(ag.store, i)
                    if str.has_prefix(k, KEY_RUN) {
                        if is_latest_of_any(ag, k) == false {
                            kv.batch_del(bt, k)
                            n_del = n_del + 1
                            excess = excess - 1
                        }
                    }
                }
                i = i + 1
            }
            if n_del > 0 {
                var wrote: bool = true
                kv.commit(ag.store, bt) catch { wrote = false }
                if wrote { kv.sync(ag.store) catch nothing() }
                if wrote {
                    m.pruned = n_del
                    metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_pruned, n_del)
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if ag.compact_after > 0 {
        if kv.dead_bytes(ag.store) >= ag.compact_after {
            let before: i64 = kv.dead_bytes(ag.store)
            let n: i64 = kv.compact(f, ag.a, ag.store) catch 0 - 1
            if n >= 0 {
                m.compacted = true
                m.reclaimed = before
                metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_compactions, 1)
            }
        }
    }
    return m
}

// Compact now, whatever the threshold — `jagent compact`. Returns the dead bytes reclaimed,
// or -1 when the store is closed or the compaction failed.
pub fn compact_now(mut ag: Agent, mut f: fs.Fs) -> i64 {
    if kv.is_open(ag.store) == false { return 0 - 1 }
    let before: i64 = kv.dead_bytes(ag.store)
    let n: i64 = kv.compact(f, ag.a, ag.store) catch 0 - 1
    if n < 0 { return 0 - 1 }
    metrics.inc(ag.m, ag.m_compactions, 1)
    return before
}

// What `jagent status` prints: the configuration, the store, every job with its last run.
pub fn render_status(mut ag: Agent, mut dst: String) {
    string_push(dst, "config=")
    string_push(dst, string_view(ag.cfg_name))
    string_push(dst, " valid=")
    push_bool(dst, ag.valid)
    string_push(dst, " generation=")
    push_i64(dst, ag.generation)
    string_push(dst, " faults=")
    push_i64(dst, ag.fault_count)
    string_push(dst, "\nstore=")
    string_push(dst, string_view(ag.store_path))
    string_push(dst, " open=")
    push_bool(dst, kv.is_open(ag.store))
    string_push(dst, " runs=")
    push_i64(dst, last_seq(ag))
    string_push(dst, "\nserver host=")
    if host_is_loopback(ag) { string_push(dst, "127.0.0.1") } else { string_push(dst, "0.0.0.0") }
    string_push(dst, " port=")
    push_i64(dst, port(ag))
    string_push(dst, " tls=")
    push_bool(dst, tls_wanted(ag))
    string_push(dst, " auth=")
    if token_required(ag) { string_push(dst, "bearer") } else { string_push(dst, "none") }
    string_push(dst, "\n")
    render_health(ag, dst)
    string_push(dst, "jobs=")
    push_i64(dst, job_count(ag) as i64)
    string_push(dst, "\n")
    var i: usize = 0
    let n: usize = job_count(ag)
    for i < n {
        string_push(dst, "  ")
        render_job_line(ag, i, dst)
        string_push(dst, "    last=")
        var nm: String = string_new()
        string_push(nm, job_name(ag, i))
        render_last(ag, string_view(nm), dst)
        string_free(nm)
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// One run, as the CLI and the API report it.
pub fn render_run(mut ag: Agent, read name: str, read r: Run, mut dst: String) {
    string_push(dst, "job=")
    string_push(dst, name)
    string_push(dst, " status=")
    string_push(dst, status_name(r.status))
    if r.status == RUN_NO_SUCH_JOB {
        string_push(dst, "\n")
        return
    }
    string_push(dst, " code=")
    push_i64(dst, r.code as i64)
    string_push(dst, " seq=")
    push_i64(dst, r.seq)
    string_push(dst, " recorded=")
    push_bool(dst, r.recorded)
    string_push(dst, " started_at=")
    push_i64(dst, r.started_at)
    string_push(dst, " duration_ms=")
    push_i64(dst, r.duration_ms)
    string_push(dst, " out_bytes=")
    push_i64(dst, r.out_bytes)
    if r.attempts > 1 {
        string_push(dst, " attempts=")
        push_i64(dst, r.attempts as i64)
    }
    string_push(dst, " output=")
    push_quoted(dst, str.trim_end(string_view(ag.preview)))
    let ev: str = string_view(ag.why)
    if ev.len > 0 {
        string_push(dst, " error=")
        push_quoted(dst, ev)
    }
    string_push(dst, "\n")
}

// ---- the HTTP API ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Handlers are `std/httpd`'s fn-pointer-plus-context shape, and the context is a `Serve`:
// the agent's cell, the spawner and the clock a `POST …/run` spends. The agent itself is
// reached through its cell BY ADDRESS — `(p + 0).*` in argument position passes the place,
// not a copy — so a handler mutates the one agent every other handler sees.

pub struct Serve {
    ag: *mut Agent,
    sp: sysproc.Spawner,
    blk: time.Clock,
}

// The context every handler is given. `cell` must hold the agent for as long as the server
// runs; the caller frees the context with `free_serve` after `httpd.stop`.
pub fn serve_ctx(cell: *mut Agent, take sp: sysproc.Spawner, take blk: time.Clock) -> *mut Serve {
    var p: *mut Serve = alloc(Serve, 1)
    unsafe { (p + 0).* = Serve{ ag: cell, sp: sp, blk: blk } }
    return p
}

pub fn free_serve(p: *mut Serve) {
    free_ptr(p as *mut u8)
}

fn ctx_of(ctx: *mut u8) -> *mut Serve {
    return ctx as *mut Serve
}

fn count_request(p: *mut Serve) {
    var s: Serve = unsafe { (p + 0).* }
    unsafe { metrics.inc((s.ag + 0).*.m, (s.ag + 0).*.m_http, 1) }
}

// The gate, as `std/httpd` middleware: it runs BEFORE routing, so a request without the
// token learns nothing about the route table (401, not 404). With no token configured it
// passes everything; with one, every request must carry `Authorization: Bearer <token>`
// except `GET /health` — a readiness probe that reveals only the service's phase, and the
// one thing a load balancer must be able to ask without a secret. The comparison is
// constant-time in the token's length; a refusal is counted (`jagent_http_unauthorized`)
// and never counted as a routed request.
fn mw_auth(ctx: *mut u8, mut x: httpd.Exchange) -> i32 {
    let p: *mut Serve = ctx_of(ctx)
    var s: Serve = unsafe { (p + 0).* }
    var need: bool = false
    unsafe { need = token_required((s.ag + 0).*) }
    if need == false { return httpd.HTTPD_NEXT }
    if str_eq(httpd.method(x), "GET") and str_eq(httpd.path(x), "/health") { return httpd.HTTPD_NEXT }
    let h: str = httpd.header(x, "authorization")
    var fine: bool = false
    if str.has_prefix(h, "Bearer ") {
        unsafe { fine = token_matches((s.ag + 0).*, h[7 .. h.len]) }
    }
    if fine { return httpd.HTTPD_NEXT }
    unsafe { metrics.inc((s.ag + 0).*.m, (s.ag + 0).*.m_unauth, 1) }
    httpd.set_header(x, "WWW-Authenticate", "Bearer realm=\"jagent\"")
    httpd.respond(x, 401, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", "unauthorized: this API needs `Authorization: Bearer <agent.token>`\n")
    return httpd.HTTPD_DONE
}

fn h_health(ctx: *mut u8, mut x: httpd.Exchange) -> i32 {
    let p: *mut Serve = ctx_of(ctx)
    count_request(p)
    var s: Serve = unsafe { (p + 0).* }
    var body: String = string_new()
    unsafe { render_health((s.ag + 0).*, body) }
    var live: bool = false
    unsafe { live = is_live((s.ag + 0).*) }
    var st: i32 = 200
    if live == false { st = 503 }
    httpd.respond(x, st, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", string_view(body))
    string_free(body)
    return 0
}

fn h_metrics(ctx: *mut u8, mut x: httpd.Exchange) -> i32 {
    let p: *mut Serve = ctx_of(ctx)
    count_request(p)
    var s: Serve = unsafe { (p + 0).* }
    var body: String = string_new()
    unsafe { render_metrics((s.ag + 0).*, body) }
    httpd.respond(x, 200, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", string_view(body))
    string_free(body)
    return 0
}

fn h_jobs(ctx: *mut u8, mut x: httpd.Exchange) -> i32 {
    let p: *mut Serve = ctx_of(ctx)
    count_request(p)
    var s: Serve = unsafe { (p + 0).* }
    var body: String = string_new()
    unsafe { render_jobs((s.ag + 0).*, body) }
    httpd.respond(x, 200, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", string_view(body))
    string_free(body)
    return 0
}

fn respond_no_job(mut x: httpd.Exchange, read name: str) {
    var body: String = string_new()
    string_push(body, "no such job: ")
    string_push(body, name)
    string_push(body, "\n")
    httpd.respond(x, 404, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", string_view(body))
    string_free(body)
}

fn h_job(ctx: *mut u8, mut x: httpd.Exchange) -> i32 {
    let p: *mut Serve = ctx_of(ctx)
    count_request(p)
    var s: Serve = unsafe { (p + 0).* }
    var nm: String = string_new()
    string_push(nm, httpd.param(x, "name"))
    var body: String = string_new()
    var found: bool = false
    unsafe { found = render_job((s.ag + 0).*, string_view(nm), body) }
    if found {
        httpd.respond(x, 200, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", string_view(body))
    } else {
        respond_no_job(x, string_view(nm))
    }
    string_free(body)
    string_free(nm)
    return 0
}

// `POST /jobs/:name/run`. Any other method is 405 — the route is registered for every
// method so the answer can be the right one rather than a routing 404.
fn h_run(ctx: *mut u8, mut x: httpd.Exchange) -> i32 {
    let p: *mut Serve = ctx_of(ctx)
    count_request(p)
    var s: Serve = unsafe { (p + 0).* }
    if str_eq(httpd.method(x), "POST") == false {
        httpd.set_header(x, "Allow", "POST")
        httpd.respond(x, 405, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", "use POST to run a job\n")
        return 0
    }
    var nm: String = string_new()
    string_push(nm, httpd.param(x, "name"))
    var ji: i32 = 0 - 1
    unsafe { ji = find_job((s.ag + 0).*, string_view(nm)) }
    if ji < 0 {
        respond_no_job(x, string_view(nm))
        string_free(nm)
        return 0
    }
    var r: Run = no_run()
    unsafe { r = run_job((s.ag + 0).*, s.sp, s.blk, string_view(nm)) }
    // The spawner's counters and the clock's reads moved; put them back.
    unsafe { (p + 0).* = s }
    var body: String = string_new()
    unsafe { render_run((s.ag + 0).*, string_view(nm), r, body) }
    httpd.respond(x, 200, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", string_view(body))
    string_free(body)
    string_free(nm)
    return 0
}

const JAGENT_LOG_LIMIT: usize = 20

fn h_logs(ctx: *mut u8, mut x: httpd.Exchange) -> i32 {
    let p: *mut Serve = ctx_of(ctx)
    count_request(p)
    var s: Serve = unsafe { (p + 0).* }
    var body: String = string_new()
    unsafe { render_logs((s.ag + 0).*, "", JAGENT_LOG_LIMIT, body) }
    httpd.respond(x, 200, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", string_view(body))
    string_free(body)
    return 0
}

fn h_logs_of(ctx: *mut u8, mut x: httpd.Exchange) -> i32 {
    let p: *mut Serve = ctx_of(ctx)
    count_request(p)
    var s: Serve = unsafe { (p + 0).* }
    var nm: String = string_new()
    string_push(nm, httpd.param(x, "name"))
    var ji: i32 = 0 - 1
    unsafe { ji = find_job((s.ag + 0).*, string_view(nm)) }
    if ji < 0 {
        respond_no_job(x, string_view(nm))
        string_free(nm)
        return 0
    }
    var body: String = string_new()
    unsafe { render_logs((s.ag + 0).*, string_view(nm), JAGENT_LOG_LIMIT, body) }
    httpd.respond(x, 200, "text/plain; charset=utf-8", string_view(body))
    string_free(body)
    string_free(nm)
    return 0
}

// Register the API on `sv`:
//
//   GET  /health            the service's two questions
//   GET  /metrics           every counter, gauge and histogram, name-ordered
//   GET  /jobs              the job table
//   GET  /jobs/:name        one job and its last run (404 for an unknown name)
//   POST /jobs/:name/run    run it now; the run's summary (404 unknown, 405 other methods)
//   GET  /logs              the last 20 runs, oldest first
//   GET  /logs/:name        the last 20 runs of one job (404 for an unknown name)
//
// Plain text, one fact per line; a client that wants JSON reads the store's records,
// which already are. When `agent.token` is set, every route but `GET /health` is behind
// the bearer-token middleware (`mw_auth`).
pub fn install(mut sv: httpd.Server, p: *mut Serve) {
    let c: *mut u8 = p as *mut u8
    httpd.use_middleware(sv, httpd.handler(c, &mw_auth))
    httpd.route(sv, "GET", "/health", httpd.handler(c, &h_health))
    httpd.route(sv, "GET", "/metrics", httpd.handler(c, &h_metrics))
    httpd.route(sv, "GET", "/jobs", httpd.handler(c, &h_jobs))
    httpd.route(sv, "GET", "/jobs/:name", httpd.handler(c, &h_job))
    httpd.route(sv, "*", "/jobs/:name/run", httpd.handler(c, &h_run))
    httpd.route(sv, "GET", "/logs", httpd.handler(c, &h_logs))
    httpd.route(sv, "GET", "/logs/:name", httpd.handler(c, &h_logs_of))
}
